Round R06 (Paper A). Multi-expert review of the paper blueprint before drafting. Read-only.
Play four roles in turn and label each answer: [EDITOR] SUSCOM handling editor; [DOMAIN] carbon-aware computing and
HPC scheduling referee; [STATS] statistics and reproducibility referee; [WRITER] senior author who has published many
papers in this journal.

Read paper/SPEC.md (the blueprint: titles, claims, outline, equations, algorithms, figures, numbers register, open
items), research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md (v1, journal rules and personas), research/journal/LESSONS.md (L1-L8),
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md (overlap with SUSCOM 101470 ReBase, 101498 and others), research/P4_PLAN.md (v2)
and research/P4_PLAN_X.md (X1-X7). You reviewed the frozen design in R03-R05.

Questions (at most 5 lines per role per question):
1. What is the single most important finding this paper can honestly lead with, and does the SPEC lead with it?
   Propose the abstract's first two sentences and the title (finding-led, no negatives, <= 16 words, true on all three
   traces; the runtime term exceeds the forecast term on PM100 and KIT FH2 but not on F-DATA).
2. Which planned claim is weakest, and how should it be worded or cut?
3. What is missing that a referee will demand, and which of X1-X7 / S1-S5 should be in the main text versus the
   supplement?
4. Structure: section order, length, 5 algorithms, 13-14 figures, about 68 references: keep or change?
5. Anything that would make you reject.
End with: VERDICT: DRAFT AS PLANNED | DRAFT AFTER CHANGES (list) | REPLAN (reason); confidence 0-1.

