## 1. Working title (OWNER TO APPROVE; the text uses the macro `\papertitle`)

From LESSONS L8 (word counts by A17); both are at most 16 words (W1), neither contains a number, a question mark or a method-name colon, and neither says that runtime matters more than the forecast in general (W25):

1. OWNER TO APPROVE: "Forecast and walltime information jointly determine the carbon saving of delay-scheduled HPC batch jobs" (14 words). Finding-led, true on all three traces (both Shapley terms are positive and the interaction is small: keys `TEST.<trace>.w.S12.shapley.forecast`, `.runtime`, `.interaction`). Untested form in SUSCOM: 0 of 16 titles are finding-led (A14; risk G4). "HPC" is defined in the abstract.
2. OWNER TO APPROVE (fallback that matches the SUSCOM norm): "Carbon savings of delay-based HPC batch scheduling with past-only forecasts and requested walltimes" (13 words).

Other options remain in LESSONS L8 (options 3 to 6). The title stays provisional until all analyses are done (MP s8).

## 2. The story and the three claims

One-paragraph story (DRAFT, for writers; no numbers here, numbers enter through section 3 and 5): Carbon-aware delay scheduling shifts non-interruptible batch jobs inside a slack window to hours of lower grid carbon intensity. Published studies of this lever mostly give the scheduler perfect or noise-perturbed signals and known job lengths. A deployed scheduler sees neither: it sees a past-only forecast of grid carbon intensity and the walltime the user requested, which is a loose upper bound on the actual runtime. We replay three real HPC job traces on 42 US balancing authorities (BAs) over a held-out year, with a pre-registered design that crosses the signal (perfect information against a past-only LightGBM forecast) with the runtime estimate (actual against requested), adds a history-based runtime prediction, and splits the gap between the perfect-information and the deployable scheduler with a Shapley allocation. No new scheduler is proposed (W8): the planner is the delay-to-lowest-integral policy of earlier work. The deployable scheduler keeps a minority to a half of the perfect-information saving; the requested walltime costs more saving than the forecast on two of three traces; a history predictor recovers part of that cost; and every number comes with an interval, a baseline ladder and a stated limit.

| # | Claim the paper makes | Ledger keys (PM100 first; F-DATA and KIT FH2 by the same pattern) | Strength |
|---|---|---|---|
| C1 | A deployable scheduler (past-only LightGBM forecast, requested walltime, arm D = lgbm\|req) realises a positive saving that is a minority to a half of the perfect-information saving at S = 12 h. | `TEST.PM100.w.S12.H1_deployable_saving.est/.lo/.hi/.p_holm` (N016 est with interval, N017 Holm p); `TEST.PM100.w.S12.saving.oracle\|act`; `TEST.PM100.w.S12.shapley.retained_share`; the same keys for FDATA, KIT_FH2 | H1 is confirmatory on PM100 only (PR s6). On F-DATA and KIT FH2 the same test is secondary (PR s7.6); the F-DATA and KIT FH2 H1 numbers are reported as secondary replications, never as confirmations. |
| C2 | Planning with the requested walltime instead of the actual runtime costs saving (H2), and a history-based runtime prediction recovers part of it (H3). | `TEST.PM100.w.S12.H2_runtime_penalty.est/.lo/.hi/.p_holm`; `TEST.PM100.w.S12.H3_prediction_recovery.est/.lo/.hi/.p_holm`; `TEST.PM100.w.S12.perba_H2.pos/.sig/.n`; two-stage intervals `TEST.PM100.w.twostage.S12.H2_runtime_penalty.lo/.hi` | H2 and H3 confirmatory on PM100 (Holm over H1-H3); the other traces secondary. Per-BA counts and two-stage intervals secondary (PR s7.3, s7.12). |
| C3 | The gap between perfect information and the deployable scheduler splits into a forecast term and a runtime term; the runtime term is larger on PM100 and KIT FH2, the forecast term on F-DATA. | `TEST.<trace>.w.S12.shapley.gap/.forecast/.runtime/.interaction/.retained_share`; `H4_interaction.est/.lo/.hi` | Secondary and descriptive (PR s7.1, s7.9; H4 demoted on Codex R03). Shapley intervals need a post-hoc script (FF item 2); until it exists the terms are point estimates. |
