### 4 Method (2,100 words)
Purpose: state the planner, the arms, the runtime estimates, the estimands, the inference and the registration so that a reader could re-implement them.
4.1 Planner and realised emissions (300 words): job with arrival a, requested walltime R_req, actual runtime R_act, mean power P; slack S in {2, 6, 12, 24} h (primary 12 h); candidate starts on a 0.25 h grid; the planner minimises the integral of its signal over [s, s + R_hat] and ties go to the earliest start; the planner sees 98 hourly intervals from floor(a); forecast leads 1 to 96, with intervals 96 and 97 holding the lead-96 value; jobs whose window touches an unusable hour are excluded; realised emissions are scored on the actual intensity over [s, s + R_act]. Equations E1-E4. Algorithm 1 (Plan). ref: wiesner2021, sukprasert2024limits.
4.2 Arms and the 2 x 2 design (250 words): signals oracle, persistence, seasonal naive day, seasonal naive week, profile7, LightGBM, historical profile; runtime estimates act, req, pred, pred2; D = lgbm|req (selected on DEV before TEST); non-forecast baselines run-now and night rule; Fig 1b (the 2 x 2). Table T3 (arms and estimands).
4.3 Forecasters (300 words): global LightGBM with horizon as a feature, trained on TRAIN only, never refit; causal inputs (flag 1 and 2 hours forward-filled at most 6 h); future-perturbation test: 150 tests, 0 failed; DEV skill (pooled 1 to 24 h MAE lgbm against seasonal naive day) and BA counts (38 of 42, 41 of 42) as the basis for choosing D; frozen hyperparameters without tuning (HPO sensitivity S5, exploratory). Equation E5 (forecast issue and lead holding). ref: friedman2001 (reserve), NEW: LightGBM, maji2022carboncast, hyndman2006, cerqueira2020.
4.4 Runtime estimates (200 words): pred = mean actual runtime of the user's two most recent jobs that ended before the job's submission with state COMPLETED or TIMEOUT, capped at R_req; R_req if no history; pred2 = min(R_req, 2 x pred), k = 2 fixed before TEST; leakage assertion in `src/prep_jobs.py` (1,000 jobs recomputed naively, passed). Equation E6. Algorithm 2 (Runtime estimate). ref: tsafrir2007, gaussier2015, fan2017.
4.5 Job sampling and calendar alignment (200 words): each job is placed in a random week of the period with its own local weekday and clock time in the BA's standard local time; 3 alignment replicates by 20,000 jobs per BA per trace (all jobs if fewer); seed 20261111; unweighted outcome is the per-job mean realised intensity (DEV row 1). Algorithm 3 (Calendar alignment). Equation E7.
4.6 Estimands and the Shapley split (300 words): saving(arm), pooled over BAs, days and replicates, energy-weighted; inference target is the fixed set of 42 eligible BAs over the TEST year (selected on coverage, not sampled); H1, H2, H3; retained share; Shapley allocation of the gap with the interaction shared equally; H4 descriptive. Equations E8-E13. ref: lundberg2020, owen2014.
4.7 Inference (300 words): stratified block bootstrap with all 42 BAs kept in every replicate; 7-day blocks of arrival days resampled within BA; B = 2000; seed 7 + S; one-sided null-centred p; Holm over H1-H3 at alpha = 0.05; 95 % percentile interval for every estimate; two-stage bootstrap (BAs then blocks) shown beside as a reading for a hypothetical BA population, not a second confirmation; what the interval is a statement about (fixed BAs, conditional on the observed job sample; W24). Equations E14-E16. Algorithm 4 (Evaluation and bootstrap). ref: efron1979, politis1994, NEW: Holm 1979.
4.8 Pre-registration, deviations and exploratory analyses (250 words): freeze date 2026-11-11 IST, md5, code md5s at freeze (PR header); deviations DEV rows 1 to 4; secondary analyses were computed by post-freeze code `src/secondary.py` that only reads saved results (DEV row 3); the post-freeze exploratory items S1 to S5 and X1 to X7 are named here with one sentence each, with the rule that they are reported beside the frozen values and never promoted (W11, F14). Algorithm 5 (final: end-to-end study procedure, calls Algorithms 1 to 4). ref: nosek2018.
Figures: Fig 1 (second citation), Fig 4 (worked example: one BA, one week, actual and forecast intensity at an arrival, start chosen under act and req). Tables: T3. Ledger keys: PR constants only (B, block length, replicates, seed, jobs per BA); no result numbers except the DEV skill counts (M-rows).

