# Journal expert brief, Paper A, SUSCOM (give this file to every drafting, figure and reviewer agent)

v1 written 2026-11-12 by agent A17 from v0 (A11; kept as `JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF_v0.md`), `journal/A14_STYLE_MEASURES.md` (16 papers measured), `journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md` (novelty wording) and `journal/LESSONS.md` (L1 to L8). A change log at the end lists every edit. Short names: JB = `_shared/JOURNAL_BASICS.md`; JP = `_shared/JOURNAL_PROFILE_SUSCOM.md` (P.n = its "Paywalled pass" items, which win over earlier sections where they differ); JPA = `paper_A/research/JOURNAL_PROFILE_A.md`; SP = `STYLE_PROFILE.md`; BB = `BEST_PAPER_BAR.md`; FP = `FIGURE_PLAN.md`; MP = `_shared/MEGA_PROMPT.md`; PR = `PREREG.md`; PB = `paper_B/research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md`; A13, A14, LS = the three files named above. Numbers: only ledger keys from `tables/ledger_TEST.csv` (and `ledger_TEST_*.csv`, `ledger_DEV*.csv`); never type a result. Facts not in the files are marked "not established".

## 1. The paper in five lines
1. Question (PR s1, D0004): for non-interruptible HPC batch jobs that may be delayed inside a slack window to lower-carbon hours, how much of the perfect-information carbon saving is realised when the scheduler sees only a past-only forecast of grid carbon intensity and only the user's requested walltime (or a history-based runtime prediction)?
2. Data: EIA-930 hourly average carbon intensity, 42 balancing authorities (BAs), TRAIN 2018-07..2024-06, DEV 2024-07..2025-06, TEST 2025-07..2026-06; job traces PM100 (primary), F-DATA and KIT FH2 (secondary) (PR s2-3).
3. Design: pre-registered, frozen by md5 before TEST; 2x2 of signal (oracle, LightGBM forecast) by runtime estimate (actual, requested), plus a history predictor; simple-policy ladder; stratified block bootstrap, Holm over H1-H3; Shapley split of the oracle-to-deployable gap (PR s4-7).
4. Finding: see ledger keys `TEST.PM100.w.S12.H1_deployable_saving.*`, `...H2_runtime_penalty.*`, `...H3_prediction_recovery.*`, and the Shapley keys `TEST.PM100.w.S12.shapley`. State each with the PR s8 wording; never quote a number from this brief.
5. Why SUSCOM: lane A "making computing sustainable" (scheduling and allocation; algorithms for reduced energy for HPC; JB s2) and a niche JPA s1 found empty inside the journal (no SUSCOM paper on carbon-intensity forecasting for computing, runtime estimates in carbon-aware scheduling, or EIA-930; the search was Crossref only, so say "we found none").

## 2. Journal rules (source in brackets)
R1. Article type: "original and timely research papers and survey articles"; no formal type list. Paper A is a research paper. [JB s3]
R2. Word or page limit: none stated in the guide. Plan 9,000 to 11,000 words counted from the title to the References heading (captions and table cells included; prose alone is about 8,000 to 10,000), about 14 to 19 typeset pages, 9 to 12 figures and 6 to 9 tables; say which counting basis a quoted number uses. Do not pad above 11,000. [JB s3; A14 s4.4 change 1: median 11.1k words and 16 pages, 2026 pages 9, 16, 18, 19, 19, 32; the prose-versus-total split is A17's reading]
R3. Abstract: at most 250 words, stand-alone, no references, abbreviations defined; working range 200 to 230 words, one paragraph. [JB s3; JP P.2 item 2, P.5 item 1] Count on the Word render. (STYLE_PROFILE and BB say 220 to 250; the controller's update supersedes: stay at or below 230.) Note: 5 of 15 typeset abstracts are at or above 250 words (101398 275, 100592 265, 101216 256, 100985 251, 101470 250), so the limit is not a safety margin and is not enforced at typeset stage; keep Paper A at 200 to 230. [A14 s4.4 change 2]
R4. Highlights: required at submission; 3 to 5 bullets; at most 85 characters each including spaces; separate editable file with "highlights" in the file name; do not embed in the manuscript body. [JB s3; JP P.2 item 14, P.5 item 6]
R5. Keywords: 1 to 7, English, avoid multi-word keywords containing "and" or "of"; aim at 5 to 6. [JB s3; JP P.2 item 4]
R6. Graphical abstract: optional; separate file at least 1328 px wide, readable at 13 cm. Decision on whether to make one: open (not established that it helps).
R7. Source files: .docx or .tex, PDF not accepted as source. Template: the guide names none; elsarticle is the Elsevier default (JP s3 tags it not verified). Follow elsarticle and say so. [JB s3; PB s0 rule 6]
R8. Numbered sections 1, 1.1; abstract unnumbered; acknowledgements directly before references; appendices A, B with Eq. (A.1). [JB s3]
R9. References: numbered in square brackets in order of appearance; include DOIs; mark preprints "preprint"; cite datasets with [dataset] and software as references; journal names per LTWA at proof. Target 40 to 55 (median 36 to 42, range 21 to 78). Every DOI a blue clickable link (owner rule, D0012). [JB s3; JP P.2 item 9, P.5 item 2; progress.md D0012] Note: SUSCOM house style prints DOIs in only 6 of 16 reference lists, so DOIs are an owner-rule addition, not a journal requirement; target 40 to 55 (median 49, topic-close 40.5) stands. [A14 change 3]
R10. Declarations: competing-interest file from the Elsevier declarations tool (separate upload); funding sentence (exact no-funding wording in JB s4, `[USER]` flag until the owner says otherwise); CRediT (14 roles) after the Conclusion and before References. [JB s4; MP s12]
R11. Generative-AI declaration: ONLY in its own section before the reference list, with the Elsevier template title and sentence verbatim (JB s4). Never in Methods, Acknowledgements, cover letter or title page. Scope wording: confirm with the owner; never change a statement the owner or professor has edited. [JB s4; MP s12; owner rule, D0012] Note: none of the 16 typeset papers (six from 2026) shows an AI-declaration section or title line, so the wording cannot be checked against a published example; use the Elsevier template wording from JB s4 and keep the section because the owner rule requires it. [A14 change 4]
R12. Data availability: Option C. Deposit and link: Zenodo DOI for code, derived data, ledger, PREREG and `SHA256SUMS.txt`; state source licences (EIA-930 public domain; PM100 and F-DATA CC-BY-4.0; KIT FH2 licence terms from the Parallel Workloads Archive: check before release). The owner uploads Zenodo manually from our guide; use placeholders until the DOI exists. Never leave the section empty. [JB s4; JP s12, P.5 item 9; PR s2; D0012]
R13. Code availability: private GitHub; the owner gives the link before any push; wording uses a placeholder until then. [D0012]
R14. Figures: separate files, vector PDF with embedded fonts plus 300 dpi PNG, colour-blind-safe, one image per file; no AI manipulation of primary images. Tables: editable text, notes below, no vertical rules or shading. [JB s3]
R15. Peer review: single anonymized, at least two reviewers; editors may desk-reject; first-decision time 1 day (publisher insights page; JIF year not verified). [JB s1]
R16. Author names, affiliations and emails stay as placeholders `[USER]`. [D0012; MP s12]

## 3. Style rules for writers
Title
W1. Title: owner wants a finding-led title with no negatives and no trailing region or dataset phrase (MP s8). SUSCOM evidence: 14 of 16 titles have at most 16 words; 0 of 16 are finding-led; 9 of 16 use a colon (5 with a method name); descriptive, verb-led and colon-subtitle forms all occur. Rule: at most 16 words, descriptive object or finding clause, no question mark, no acronym beyond HPC (define in the abstract), no method-name colon. A finding clause is allowed but untested in this journal (editor reaction not established, G4); the fallback is a verb-less descriptive title of the 101498 type. Candidates with rationale: LS L8 (six options). Titles must not say that the runtime term exceeds the forecast term in general: it does on PM100 and KIT FH2 and not on F-DATA (figures/FIGURE_FIXES item 2; ledger shapley keys). The owner approves the final title; it stays provisional until all analyses are done (MP s8). [A14 change 5; LS L8]
Abstract (200 to 230 words, one paragraph, 6 to 9 sentences)
W2. Moves in order: (1) sustainability context in one sentence, with the computing lever; (2) gap in one sentence (deployable saving is rarely scored with real forecasts and real runtime estimates); (3) design with data named (42 BAs, three job traces, held-out year, pre-registered); (4) two to four results with interval each (H1, H2, H3 and the Shapley split); (5) one honest weak or null result; (6) one implication sentence for operators. [JP P.2 item 3; SP finding 4; JPA s5] Three to five numbers; every one a ledger key. Define abbreviations at first use in the abstract (BA, HPC, EIA).
Introduction
W3. Introduction plan (LS L1): paragraph 1 states the sustainability stake in one cited figure and the computing lever; paragraph 2 maps temporal-shifting studies in one sentence with bracketed ranges and states what they assume (perfect or noise-perturbed signals, runtimes known: 101498, Wiesner 2021, Sukprasert 2024; COAST, LiteCast, LACS named); paragraph 3 says what a deployed scheduler sees (a past-only forecast and the requested walltime) and carries the gap sentence; paragraph 4 gives the design in two sentences; then 3 to 4 contributions in nominal or passive voice (3 is the median, range 2 to 5; "we" optional), the last one the release of code, derived data, ledger, pre-registration and checksum (101498 ends its list with a release item; 101106 releases code and data), then a roadmap sentence by section number that names Section 6 as Discussion with limitations. A one-paragraph definition of run-now, slack, requested walltime and the perfect-information arm sits in the Introduction (101407 does the same for its terms). Gap sentence and bullets: LS L1 and L2. [A14 change 6 as corrected by LS (101498 does end with a release bullet); A13 s3; LS L1, L2]
W4. Contribution verbs: "we quantify", "we measure", "we decompose", "we release". Banned: "novel", "first", "to the best of our knowledge", "groundbreaking". [PB s2 rule 4; JP s14 shows these as common weakness in the corpus] Also forbidden (A13 s3): "first to compare forecast-based with perfect-information carbon-aware scheduling", "first to show forecast error erodes saving", "first study on real traces and many grids", "no prior work treats runtime uncertainty" (LACS models synthetic length error), any claim about water, land use, spatial shifting or wind, and a numeric comparison to the headline figures of 101498, 101216, 101470, 101407 or 100950. 101498 uses the hedge "to the best of our knowledge"; Paper A does not; it writes "we found no study that ..." or "is not reported in the studies we reviewed". [A13 s3; LS L1.3]
Sections
W5. Order: 1 Introduction; 2 Related work (with comparison table); 3 Data; 4 Method (arms, estimands, scheduler, forecasting, inference); 5 Results (RQ-by-RQ or H1-H3, then secondaries); 6 Discussion (trade-offs, delay cost, BA character, Threats to validity, Reproducibility); 7 Conclusion; then CRediT, declarations, AI declaration section, data and code availability, references. A separate Related work section appears in 13 of 14 typeset papers. [JP P.2 items 5, 12; JPA s5]
Related work
W6. One comparison table (T1 in FP). Rows: Wiesner 2021, Sukprasert 2024, Radovanovic 2023, CarbonCast 2022 (stronger venues); SUSCOM 101498, 101216, 101470, 101407, 100950; LACS, COAST and LiteCast (outside works that bound the runtime-error and real-trace claims, NOVELTY_SCAN s.b); Paper A. Columns (A13 s4, cells read in full for the five SUSCOM papers): decision, workload, carbon or renewable signal, forecast, oracle, runtime uncertainty, evaluation scale, statistics, code. Fill cells only from what was read; "not extracted" for Wiesner, Sukprasert and Radovanovic runtime cells; "not re-audited" for LACS, COAST, LiteCast until read. Use the A13 s4 draft and the A13 s5 related-work sentences (2 to 4 per paper). [A13 s4-s5; BB B9]
W7. Positioning claims allowed (A13 s3, a measurement at scale, not a first): (1) a measurement of the share of the perfect-information saving a deployable scheduler realises on real HPC traces with a past-only forecaster on 42 US BAs and a pre-registered held-out year; (2) the saving lost when the planner uses the requested walltime, and the saving a history prediction recovers; (3) a 2 x 2 design with a Shapley allocation of the gap into forecast and runtime terms; (4) results beside non-forecast baselines and several forecasters under every runtime estimate with negative findings stated. Say that the forecast-to-oracle gap is known: 101470 reports it by horizon for wind power with unit tasks and no runtime (1.03 % to 21.88 % in its Table 8), Wiesner and Sukprasert use error scenarios; Paper A's value is a real past-only forecaster, real jobs and a second information factor. "None of the five SUSCOM papers" is a statement about those five only; extend nowhere without LACS, COAST and LiteCast named. [A13 s2-s3]
W8. The scheduler is the delay-to-lowest-integral policy of Wiesner and Sukprasert; say that no new scheduler is proposed. [PR s7 scope note]
Threats and reproducibility
W9. Limitations and threats to validity: heading "Limitations and threats to validity" (precedent: Limitations three times, Threats to validity once; 4 of 6 articles in 2026 have one), placed in or directly after Discussion. Form: run-in bold labels, each paragraph one weakness, why the result may still hold, what would test it (101498 section 5.5 pattern; LS L4.3), 400 to 600 words; the label-per-paragraph form and length are Paper A's choice. One label per item: job sample and interval scope (A08 T1; S1); data vintage and EIA publication delay (T2; S2); average versus marginal signal, unverified oil, other and unknown factors, storage and imports excluded (T3, 4a, 4b; S3); cross-system pairing of foreign traces on US grids (T4; S6); no queueing, capacity, PUE or interruptibility (T5); estimand and BA selection on TEST availability (T6, M1); delay-to-lowest-integral heuristic and a relative oracle (T7); KIT FH2 1 kW and status limits, unscored forecaster tuning until S5 is done, cleaned-hour counts and the TEST-year arrival gap (M2, M3, M7, D0012). [A14 change 7; A08 s2-s3; LS L4.3]
W10. Reproducibility subsection: one-command rerun, frozen md5s, ledger, Zenodo placeholder (101106 has a dedicated heading). [JPA s2.2] A dedicated Reproducibility subsection occurs in 1 of 15 typeset papers (101106 5.1) and a one-line reproducibility sentence in three more, so it is a differentiator, not a conformity requirement. [A14 G8]
W11. Preregistration disclosure: state the freeze date, md5, each deviation (PREREG_DEVIATIONS.md) and that the P4 items S1 to S5 are post-freeze and exploratory, labelled as such in text, tables and captions. [PR s9; P4_PLAN]
Register and mechanics
W12. "We" for actions and decisions (Methods steps, design choices); passive for set-up and data description; 3 to 7 "we" per 1,000 words (median 3.4, topic-close 3.75, 8 or more in 2 of 15; 101498 at 6.9 is the closest exemplar); at most 30 % passive (not re-measured, A14 s5). [A14 change 8; JP s14; SP finding 2]
W13. Sentences: mean 22 to 25 words, none above 40, hard cap 45; one claim per sentence. [SP finding 1; MP s9]
W14. No em or en dashes (use commas, colons, "to"). One English variant: American (measured in the SUSCOM paper and all four stronger-venue texts); e.g. "data center". [JP s14; SP finding 6]
W15. Hedging about 1 per 100 words, on mechanisms and interpretations only, never on measured numbers. [SP finding 3]
W16. Every number is a ledger key; a missing key is an error; numbers identical across abstract, highlights, results, conclusion, cover letter. [CLAUDE.md evidence rule 1; MP s11]
W17. Every claim about practice (EIA publication delay, HPC user behaviour with walltimes, grid mixes) carries a citation or is softened. [MP s11]
W18. Show absolute (kgCO2 or g per job-hour) and relative effects with intervals; relative-only reporting is a flagged weakness. [BB B10]
W19. Describe each result with the PR s8 wording; secondary and post-freeze items are never called confirmatory; H4 is descriptive (demoted on Codex R03).
W20. No disparaging wording about prior work; negative and null results stated plainly. [CLAUDE.md rule 6]
W21. Define each abbreviation again at first use in the body; keep arm labels exactly: oracle|act, oracle|req, lgbm|act, lgbm|req (D), lgbm|pred, lgbm|pred2, run-now, night, persistence, seasonal naive day and week, profile7, histprofile. [FP; PR s4]
W22. Definitions, in the Introduction or Section 3 (LS L6): average (production-based, location-based Scope 2) carbon intensity as the accounted signal, marginal intensity named as the decision-relevant signal that is not modelled; slack S (maximum start delay; "delay tolerance" and "flexibility window" only as synonyms in Related work); requested walltime (the limit the user supplies, enforced by killing the job, state TIMEOUT) versus actual runtime; perfect-information arm and "oracle" (the same planner given the realised intensity, not a global optimum); deployable (uses only what is known at submission); balancing authority (BA) at first use; run-now as the carbon-agnostic baseline. Never write that the average-versus-marginal choice leaves relative comparisons unchanged (101398 does; A08 T3 says the effect is unknown); cite Sukprasert for the comparison and point to S3. [LS L6; A08 T3; 101398 s3.3.2]
W23. Units and wording of effects: saving is relative (1 minus emissions over run-now emissions); differences of savings are percentage points (pp); penalty and recovery are pp; retained share is a ratio (`shapley.retained_share`, deployable over perfect-information saving); "significant" only for the Holm-adjusted bootstrap result of H1 to H3; report p at the bootstrap floor as "p < 0.0005"; one interpretive (mechanism) sentence after each estimate, hedged on the mechanism only. [LS L3.1, L3.4, L6.6, L6.7; A08 3f]
W24. Result reporting (LS L3): the main table has row groups (perfect-information, deployable, runtime variants, baselines) like 101470 Tables 5, 8, 9; every headline estimate carries a 95 % interval with its type, an absolute effect (g CO2 per kWh of job energy, from a ledger key to be added: run-now mean intensity per trace, A17 gap G12) and the relative effect; all three traces side by side; small and negative baselines (night rule, persistence) stay in the table; one sentence says what the interval is a statement about (the fixed 42 BAs, conditional on the observed job sample; S1 beside it, labelled exploratory). Retained share is paired with the absolute oracle saving. [LS L3.1-L3.9; A14 s4.5]
W25. Per-trace sentences must follow the ledger: the runtime term exceeds the forecast term on PM100 and KIT FH2 and not on F-DATA (keys `TEST.<trace>.w.S12.shapley.runtime` and `.forecast`); H1 to H3 are confirmatory on PM100 only (PREREG s6) and supported there (Holm p keys); on F-DATA and KIT FH2 the same contrasts are secondary replications and are described as such, never as confirmed (controller fix 04:40, flagged by A18); write "on two of three traces", never "runtime matters more than the forecast" without the trace. [LS L3.6; figures/FIGURE_FIXES item 2]
W26. Discussion structure (LS L4.1-L4.2): 6.1 what the 2 x 2 shows; 6.2 implications for operators (log requested and actual runtime, add a history-based predictor), users (walltime accuracy has a carbon value; a limit set too low kills the job), scheduler designers (forecast and runtime are separate levers; the planner is the delay-to-lowest-integral policy, no scheduler is ranked) and signal providers (data latency); 6.3 comparison with earlier work (why 12 h slack on a fixed grid gives smaller savings than the spatial or wind-driven studies; the single-case 101407 headline versus pooled 42 BAs); 6.4 limitations and threats (W9); 6.5 reproducibility (W10). Implications are counterfactual: "would" and "could", never "will be realised". [LS L4; A13 s6; EDITOR R05 q4]
W27. Adopt from earlier work (A13 s6) and say so in text: (1) report the gap and retained share by slack, parallel to 101470's gap by horizon; (2) name oracle|req as the walltime-only cost, parallel to the forecast-only gap of 101470; (3) an injected-error sweep (101216 Fig. 11) only as exploratory X2 (LS L7), never confirmatory; (4) keep run-now and night rule in T4 beside the deployable arm; (5) cite 101498's "runtimes could come from previous runs" as the position the pred arm tests. Answer in Discussion: 101216's zero-error effect has no analogue because the planner is one-stage; code and data release (101470, 101216, 101407, 100950 do not release) mentioned once. [A13 s6]

## 4. Figure and table rules
F1. Count: plan 9 to 12 figures and 6 to 9 tables (topic-close median 9.5 figures and 7.5 tables; overall 11 and 9; the ceiling is set by new information: 101429 has 41 figures with the same plot repeated for modes A, B and C). The project rule is 13 to 14 figures (FP has 14): decision for the owner (G11). Recommendation: 13, by merging FP 11 and 12 into one two-panel figure; 12 if FP 13 is also folded into Figure 6. Keep each figure only if it carries new information. [A14 change 9; LS L5; CLAUDE.md MP s7]
F2. Okabe-Ito, one fixed colour per arm family across all figures (FP style line); baselines grey; matplotlib plus seaborn via the `dataviz` skill; no saturated defaults. [MP s7; JP s9]
F3. Widths 90, 140 or 190 mm; text at least 7 pt at print size; 300 dpi PNG plus vector PDF with embedded fonts; one image per file. [MP s7; JB s3]
F4. Multi-panel figures: (a), (b) lettering, panel titles naming the trace or BA group, shared legend placed off the data, units in every axis label, one unit per axis (no "%" with "g/kWh" on one axis). [JP P.2 items 10-11]
F5. Uncertainty visible: 95 % intervals on every headline estimate. State of the field: 5 of 10 topic-close papers show some spread (101216 t-interval in a figure; 101398 A� SD and a Bonferroni test table; 101407 a CI over n = 3; 101470 A� SD over five seeds; 101429 Monte Carlo n = 30), 0 of 10 use a bootstrap, a pre-registered interval type or a hypothesis structure, and 1 of 10 reports p-values (101398). Paper A is ahead on interval type and pre-registration, not on showing a spread at all. [A14 change 10]
F6. Captions: a short title clause, one sentence stating the finding, then n (BAs, jobs) and the interval type, then "Exploratory, post-freeze" where applicable; figure captions at most 35 words and table captions at most 35 words (topic-close figure captions already run 18 to 35 words, up to 91 in 101106; table captions median 34; two papers state findings with numbers in captions: 101498, 101398). Current `figures/CAPTIONS.md` exceeds the cap in 8 of 10 captions (Figure 1 has 78 words; A17 count). [A14 change 11; LS L5.6]
F7. Every figure cited in order in the text; every figure viewed at print size by the controller before inclusion; no overlaps, even spacing. [MP s7; CLAUDE rule]
F8. Tables: booktabs three rules, visible lines in the DOCX, no `\resizebox`, notes below, generated from the ledger by script, true minus sign. [MP s7, s11; JB s3]
F9. Main result table (T4): one row per estimand H1 to H4; columns estimate, 95 % interval and interval type (fixed-BA stratified block bootstrap), raw p, Holm p, decision, n BAs, plus the same on F-DATA and KIT FH2 in a second block; baselines beside it (run-now, night, histprofile, persistence, seasonal naive, oracle). Best value in bold, consistent decimals. [PR s6; JP P.2 item 7; BB B4-B5] Layout precedents: 101470 (row groups Proposed / Ideal (Oracle) / Baseline / Ablation, mean A� spread, "-" for deterministic rows with a note) and 101398 (best value bold, a test table); Paper A replaces A� SD with 95 % intervals and Holm-adjusted p. [A14 s4.5; LS L3.2]
F10. Figure 1 is the schematic of the 2x2 design for a computing reader, written so that no carbon-grid jargon is needed to follow it. [FP 1]
F11. Algorithm boxes appear in 8 of 15 typeset papers (topic-close 5 of 10, range 0 to 3): include one (the pre-registered simulation and bootstrap procedure) and more only where a component is introduced. Numbered equations: topic-close median 12, range 0 to 51; plan 12 to 25, numbered and referenced. [A14 change 12]
F12. Failures seen in the 16 papers, forbidden here: 3D bars (101429 Fig. 10); radar charts with translucent fills (101333 Figs 4, 7); a line drawn across categorical models (101333 Figs 3, 6); truncated bar axes (101398 Fig. 8, y axis from 75); value labels on top of error bars (101398 Fig. 9); infographic equivalents (101398 Fig. 15); a legend with more than 8 entries or dash codes that carry meaning (100950 Fig. 6); tick text under 7 pt at print size (101498 Figs 5, 11; 101333 Figs 5, 8; 101106 Figs 5 to 10); the same plot repeated for each mode (101429); significance stars with no named test (101398). Savings axes start at zero with a thin run-now line. [A14 change 13]
F13. Encoding and designs to adopt (LS L5): the 2 x 2 is encoded with two channels, colour for runtime information (actual, requested, predicted) and marker or line style for signal (open or dashed for oracle, filled or solid for LightGBM), held fixed in every figure, baselines grey with direct labels, one legend outside the data; Figure 8 follows 101470 Fig. 7 (thin line per alignment replicate plus the interval band); Figure 9 follows 101216 Fig. 6 (interval plot above, box plot below, whisker rule in the caption); Figure 7 is a waterfall from the perfect-information saving to the deployable saving on a zero-based axis; one panel structure (PM100, F-DATA, KIT FH2) in every multi-trace figure (101106 consistency). [A14 s3; LS L5.1-L5.7]
F14. Post-freeze exploratory results (S1 to S5, X1 to X7) appear in figures and tables only in a visibly separate block labelled "post-freeze, exploratory", beside the frozen value, never in the main confirmatory panel. [PR s9; P4; LS L7]

## 5. Personas for reviewer agents
### 5a. SUSCOM handling editor (decision within about a day)
Checks first: scope (computing for or of sustainability), importance of the finding, clarity for a computing reader, declarations complete.
Likely rejection reasons: reads as energy-systems rather than computing (the owner's earlier desk rejection elsewhere was scope only, JP s16 item 6); incremental scheduling benchmark; missing declarations or empty data statement; overclaim.
| # | Question | Where answered |
|---|---|---|
| E1 | Why SUSCOM and not an energy-systems journal? | Abstract sentence 1; Intro para 1; scope block "scheduling and allocation", HPC algorithms |
| E2 | What is the one finding to remember? | Title; abstract moves 3-4; highlights 1-2; contribution 1 |
| E3 | Is it new relative to Wiesner, Sukprasert and the SUSCOM papers? | Intro gap sentence; T1 |
| E4 | Which SUSCOM papers does it cite and position against? | Related work, T1 (101498, 101216, 101470, 101407, 100950) |
| E5 | Open data and code with licences? | Data and code availability; Zenodo placeholder |
| E6 | Abstract at most 250 words; highlights 3 to 5 at most 85 characters; keywords at most 7? | R3-R5; Word render count |
| E7 | AI declaration in its own section only? | R11 |
| E8 | Any "novel", "first", unsupported practice claim? | W4, W17 |
| E9 | Does the title, abstract and Introduction avoid "first" and unsupported "none"? | W4; A13 s3 wording list |
### 5b. Domain referee: carbon-aware computing and HPC scheduling
Checks first: realism of the job model and signal; whether the baseline ladder includes the simple policies that Sukprasert finds capture most gain; average versus marginal intensity; whether results depend on one trace.
Likely rejection reasons: average intensity answers a marginal question; delay-only, no interruptibility, no capacity limit; US grids with Japanese, Italian and German traces; requested-walltime semantics (censoring at the limit, killed jobs) not audited; improvement reported relative to run-now only; EIA publication delay ignored. Attacks added from A13 and NOVELTY_SCAN: (i) "ReBase (101470) already scores a trained forecaster against a perfect-information Oracle by horizon" (true; it uses wind power, unit tasks, a fixed carbon factor, no runtime, one information factor); (ii) "LACS models job-length error, COAST uses an HPC trace with known runtimes, LiteCast runs a real forecast on the KIT-FH2 log with ideal job lengths"; (iii) "101498 already has real traces and four of your grids" (synthetic arrivals, one week per season, noise as forecast, no oracle, runtimes assumed known); (iv) "EuroSys 2024 and LiteCast already show forecast error costs little"; (v) "a 12 h slack gives far smaller savings than the 20 to 85 % in the spatial literature"; (vi) "a CI over three archetypes (101407) is what you replace with a bootstrap: is yours over jobs?".
| # | Question | Where answered |
|---|---|---|
| D1 | Is saving measured against run-now and against the best simple policy? | T4 baselines; PR s7 item 5; BB B5 |
| D2 | Why average and not marginal intensity; are factors defensible? | Method; threats (a); sensitivity S3 (exploratory) |
| D3 | Are walltime semantics audited (status codes, kill-at-limit share, requested to actual ratio)? | Data section; A07_JOBS.md; near-limit sensitivity (PR s7 item 11) |
| D4 | Do foreign traces on US grids bias the result? | Results by trace; threats (b); S6 limitation text |
| D5 | Is forecast error real and past-only; was the forecaster tuned fairly? | Method s4.x; forecast-skill figure; HPO sensitivity S5 labelled exploratory |
| D6 | What does the saving cost in delay? | FP 13 delay-cost figure |
| D7 | Does the 2x2 conclusion hold across slack 2, 6, 12, 24 h and by BA character? | FP 8, 11, 12 |
| D8 | EIA data delay: does the planner see data it could not have? | Threats (a); S2 sensitivity |
| D9 | Is ReBase's oracle gap not the same result? | Intro gap sentence; T1 row 101470; W7: known gap by horizon for wind and unit tasks, no runtime term; Paper A adds a second information factor and real jobs |
| D10 | How does it differ from LACS, COAST and LiteCast? | T1 rows; W7; LACS synthetic length error, COAST known runtimes and actual carbon, LiteCast ideal job lengths (re-read their full texts before submission, G10) |
| D11 | Why smaller than 20 to 85 % elsewhere? | Discussion 6.3 (W26): temporal lever, fixed grid, 12 h slack, no wind or spatial freedom; no numeric comparison (W4) |
| D12 | Do relative comparisons survive a marginal signal? | W22 (not claimed); S3 and X7 labelled exploratory; threats label on signal |
| D13 | Is runtime knowledge a realistic input? | Pred arm: previous runs of the same user, causal assertion in `src/prep_jobs.py`; H3; X1 curve (exploratory) |
### 5c. Statistics and reproducibility referee
Checks first: unit of inference, preregistration and freeze, multiplicity, dependence handling, leakage.
Likely rejection reasons: intervals that ignore job-sampling variance (A08 threat; S1); inference target unclear (fixed 42 BAs versus a population); post-freeze analyses presented as confirmatory; one-sided p with a null-centred bootstrap not explained.
| # | Question | Where answered |
|---|---|---|
| S1 | Unit of inference and target? | PR s5-6: the fixed set of 42 BAs; two-stage bootstrap shown beside as a population reading |
| S2 | Freeze, md5, TEST scored once; deviations? | Method; PREREG_DEVIATIONS.md; W11 |
| S3 | Bootstrap design (7-day blocks within BA, B = 2000, null-centred one-sided p), Holm over H1-H3? | PR s6; equations and algorithm |
| S4 | Leakage: forecasts causal, user history only from jobs ended before submission, HPO on TRAIN only? | Method; assertions in `src/prep_jobs.py`; PR s3 |
| S5 | Are the intervals complete (alignment replicates, job sample)? | S1 sensitivity; disclose if not run |
| S6 | Rerun? | Reproducibility subsection; Zenodo; ledger keys |
| S7 | Effect sizes and intervals for every headline, absolute and relative? | T4; W18 |
| S8 | Is the interval a statement about the job sample? | Method and Limitations (W24): conditional on the observed job sample; S1 labelled exploratory |
| S9 | p reported at the floor? | W23: "p < 0.0005"; one-sided null-centred explained; Holm over H1 to H3 only |
### 5d. Desk-reject screen (run before every submission candidate)
- [ ] Title at most 16 words, no "first", no claim that runtime beats the forecast in general (W1, W25).
- [ ] Abstract at most 250 words (target 200 to 230); sentence 1 states the sustainability outcome; numbers match the ledger.
- [ ] Intro cites and positions against 101498, 101216, 101470, 101407, 100950 and the stronger venues.
- [ ] Highlights 3 to 5, each at most 85 characters; keywords 1 to 7.
- [ ] Data and code statements non-empty (placeholders allowed pre-release, real DOI before submission).
- [ ] AI declaration only in its own section; competing-interest file; CRediT; funding sentence.
- [ ] No "novel", "first", unsupported practice claims; no em or en dashes; American English.
- [ ] Limitations and threats subsection present with run-in labels (W9); definitions paragraph present (W22); T1 has the A13 columns (W6).
- [ ] Figures: two-channel encoding, zero-based savings axes, captions at most 35 words, none of the F12 failures.
- [ ] Every result has a baseline and an interval; negative results present.

