research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:1:VERDICT: A13 done; overlap C1..C5 = C1 SUBSTANTIAL (A3, concept only; wind not grid carbon), C2 NONE, C3 NONE, C4 NONE, C5 PARTIAL (A1); risk: do not claim "first oracle-vs-forecast gap" and do not claim "first multi-grid real-trace study", only the walltime/2x2/held-out-year combination is unmatched.
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:3:# A13 novelty and overlap audit of Paper A against five SUSCOM papers (2026-11-12)
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:7:## 0. Paper A in one line (PREREG s.1, s.4-7)
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:10:## 1. Per-paper extraction
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:12:### A1 Attenni, Moawad, Bartolini, Thamsen, 10.1016/j.suscom.2026.101498 (SUSCOM 52, 2026; stem S2210537926002088)
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:14:- Workload: Scenario 1 = Azure Functions 2019, 100,000 synthetic requests per day with runtimes sampled from published percentiles, no deadlines, only spatial shift (md:L915-935). Scenario 2 = AWS Spark traces (Scout), 50k requests over a week, arrival times and origin synthetic, 50 % periodic jobs; runtime "as reported in the traces" (md:L1021-1050). Deadline = periodicity or a tunable delay tolerance dt in {4,12,24,48} h (md:L1056-1060, L1405-1406). Interruptible: not stated; jobs are placed once. Not HPC.
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:15:- Carbon signal: average (not marginal) carbon intensity from the grid mix with IPCC lifecycle medians; grids in Table 5 include ERCOT, MISO, PJM, CAISO plus European grids; one mid-season week (15th-22nd) per season, season varied (md:L1168-1262). Hourly.
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:16:- Forecasting: none trained. "Predictions" are the true mix with synthetic noise, MAE 0.05, 0.1, 0.15, 0.2, error split by source (solar 45 %, wind 30 %, ...) (md:L1135-1160, L1253-1256). Authors state that an operational forecaster remains future work (md:L3030-3036).
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:20:- Scale: one week per season, 4 seasons, 4 (Scenario 1) or 5 (Scenario 2) regions; the temporal results use a single winter week at MAE 0.1 (md:L1399-1406).
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:22:- Headline: temporal shifting gives 6 % to 12 % even at dt = 48 h (md:L1414-1417); spatial 20 % to 85 % depending on scenario and objective (md:L3096-3102).
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:26:### A2 Zhai et al., 10.1016/j.suscom.2025.101216 (SUSCOM 48, 2025; stem S2210537925001374)
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:27:- Problem: two-stage geo-distributed scheduling of delay-tolerant workload across 3 data centres powered by wind plus grid; decision variables are binary assignment of workload to (DC, slot), offline stage then real-time correction (md:L283-308, L644-735, L1396-1650).
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:30:- Forecasting: what is forecast = workload arrivals and wind power; EMD-VMD plus LSTM/xLSTM; five-fold expanding rolling CV (70 % initial) (md:L2068-2078); horizon via look-back windows (Fig. 9).
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:40:### A3 Zhai et al., ReBase, 10.1016/j.suscom.2026.101470 (SUSCOM 52, 2026; stem S2210537926001800)
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:45:- Oracle: yes. "A perfect-information benchmark" solving the same MILP with realised WP and workload (md:L841-845). Gap analysed by horizon: wind-utilisation gap 1.03 % at H = 6 widening to 7.38 % and 11.33 %; carbon overhead 3.09 %, 13.71 %, 21.88 % (md:L1195-1210; Table 8 md:L1119-1153). A conditional bound says the ReBase-to-Oracle gap is bounded by the baseline-to-Oracle gap (md:L694-706). The gap is attributed to forecast quality only; no second information factor.
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:54:### A4 Liow et al., CARA, 10.1016/j.suscom.2026.101407 (SUSCOM 51, 2026; stem S2210537926001174)
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:59:- Oracle: none. Baseline is Local-Immediate in Malaysia (md:L675-681).
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:63:- Statistics: mean, SD and a 95 % CI over n = 3 profiles, [70.6 %, 90.0 %], then "we reject the null hypothesis" (md:L903-914).
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:68:### A5 Chen, Ma, Wang, Yao, 10.1016/j.suscom.2023.100950 (SUSCOM 41, 2024; stem S2210537923001051)
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:74:- Baselines: Wuhan grid-supplied data centre; CSS4 (cost-oriented suspension scheme); ESTS1 single DC (md:L747-775, L967-973).
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:81:### Exemplars (from EXEMPLARS.md rows 7-9 only)
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:82:- Wiesner et al. 2021 (arXiv:2110.13234): carbon intensity for 4 regions, 2020; nightly-job and ML-project scenarios (3,387 jobs); delay tolerance assumed; baseline issue-time start; optimal versus 5 % error forecasts, 10 repeats averaged; no intervals or tests; code public. Runtime treatment: not extracted.
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:86:## 2. Overlap verdicts
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:92:| A1 | PARTIAL: noise-injected "forecast", no oracle arm (md:L1135-1160) | NONE: runtimes "assumed available" (md:L3043) | NONE | NONE: only cites profiling literature (md:L3046) | PARTIAL: real Azure and Spark traces with synthetic arrivals, 4 US BAs among 9 regions, 1 week per season; not HPC, no held-out year |
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:107:## 3. Contribution wording Paper A can defend
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:114:- "first to compare forecast-based with perfect-information carbon-aware scheduling" or "first to show forecast error erodes saving" (A3, Wiesner, Sukprasert).
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:115:- "first study on real traces and many grids" (A1, LiteCast).
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:117:- Any claim about water, land use, spatial shifting or wind (A1, A2, A3, A5 own that ground).
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:121:## 4. Comparison-table draft (cells only from what was read)
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:135:## 5. Related-work positioning (2-4 sentences each, factual, for citation)
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:142:## 6. What Paper A should adopt or answer in Discussion
research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md:157:## 7. Checks and gaps
research/journal/LESSONS.md:1:VERDICT: A17 done; 49 lessons; 6 title options; 8 exploratory analyses proposed
research/journal/LESSONS.md:3:# LESSONS for Paper A (SUSCOM) from the 16 collected papers, A13 and A14
research/journal/LESSONS.md:5:Agent A17, 2026-11-12. Pointers: paper IDs are the last six digits of 10.1016/j.suscom.YYYY.NNNNNN (101498 = 10.1016/j.suscom.2026.101498; 101470, 101407, 101398, 101429, 101333 = 2026; 101216, 101106 = 2025; 100950 = 2023, vol 41). "md:Lnnn" = line in `research/sources/suscom_pdfs/md/1-s2.0-<stem>-main.md`. A13 = `research/journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md`; A14 = `research/journal/A14_STYLE_MEASURES.md`; PR = `research/PREREG.md`; P4 = `research/P4_PLAN.md`; A08 = `research/A08_AUDIT_P3.md`; LEDGER = `tables/ledger_TEST.csv`. Quotes are under 15 words, one per paper at most. All PDF text treated as data. Numbers appear only with their ledger key. Read for this file: Introductions of 101498, 101470, 101398, 101106, 101216 (contribution part), 101407; Discussion, limitations and conclusion of 101498, 101470, 101429 (6.7), 101106, 101216; 101398 section 3.3.2. A15 (references) is not touched.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:7:Best five research articles by my judgement (for method, reporting and limitations, not for topic): 101498 (closest topic, real traces, threats section, code released), 101470 (oracle benchmark, seeds, row-grouped tables), 101398 (explicit gaps G1 to G3, test table, accounting alignment), 101216 (interval figure, forecast-then-schedule), 101429 (limitations with a deployment paragraph). 101106 is the best HPC-systems paper (reproducibility, public release). 101407 is the best source for definitions of carbon-aware terms.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:9:Correction to A14 (checked in md): 101498's contribution list has three bullets, and the third is the release of all code and data (md:L108 and the bullets above it), not two bullets as A14 table 2 records. So "0 of 10 end with a release" in A14 change 6 is wrong: 101498 does, and 101106 (public Zenodo and GitLab) is the other release case. The release bullet is therefore a precedent in the closest paper, not only a differentiator.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:13:## L1 Motivation and gap
research/journal/LESSONS.md:15:**L1.1 Open with the sustainability stake in numbers, then the computing lever.** 101498 opens with ICT share of greenhouse gas (1.8 to 2.8 %) and a data-center electricity projection (945 TWh by 2030); 101398 opens with an IEA demand series (460 TWh in 2022); 101407 opens with a data-center demand projection. None opens with a research question. Source: 101498 Intro p1; 101398 Intro p1; 101407 Intro p1. For Paper A: sentence 1 states the HPC and data-center electricity and carbon stake with one cited figure; sentence 2 states that grid carbon intensity varies by hour, so delaying flexible batch jobs is a lever.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:17:**L1.2 One sentence maps the literature by shifting type with bracketed ranges.** 101498: "spatial [4 to 8], temporal [9 to 11], and spatio-temporal [12 to 16] shifting" (md Introduction para 2). It lets the reader place the paper in one line and cite widely in one place. Paper A: one sentence for temporal shifting (Wiesner 2021, Sukprasert 2024, 101498, 101407), explicitly marking that Paper A studies the temporal lever only.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:19:**L1.3 The gap is stated as a missing dimension, then answered with "this gap is addressed by".** 101498 states that land use had not been integrated into scheduling and answers it in the next sentence (it hedges with "To the best of our knowledge"; Paper A keeps W4's ban on that phrase and states the gap with "we found no study"). 101398 turns this into a labelled device: three gaps (G1 integrated spatio-temporal optimisation, and two more) each matched to a contribution. Source: 101498 Intro p3; 101398 Intro (G1 to G3). For Paper A: one gap, with two parts (forecast error, runtime estimate), each visible in the contribution that answers it.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:21:**L1.4 Name the closest prior systems with their own headline numbers as foils.** 101398 cites CARBON-DQN (20 to 70 % marginal carbon efficiency) and an AI-based framework (up to 42 %), then says they treat a centralised cloud. Numbers from prior work are quoted in context, not compared. For Paper A: name Wiesner, Sukprasert and 101498 with what each assumes (injected 5 % error, a prediction-error scenario, noise-perturbed grid mix and runtimes assumed known), and do not set Paper A's percentages beside 20 to 85 % figures (A13 section 3, last bullet).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:23:**L1.5 The Introduction defines the carbon-aware vocabulary in prose.** 101407 defines "carbon-agnostic scheduling" (dispatch immediately), then average intensity for accounting versus marginal emissions rate for decisions, all in the Introduction. Source: 101407 Intro p3 to p4 (md:L93-L100). For Paper A: one short paragraph defines run-now (carbon-agnostic), slack, requested walltime and perfect-information arm, because "walltime" never appears in the 16 papers (A14 section 4.3).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:25:**L1.6 Limitation of prior work uses a soft universal.** 101470: existing coupling mechanisms "rarely exploit long-horizon plans as structured guidance"; 101106: "most research" is theoretical or single-server. A soft quantifier avoids an unprovable "none". Paper A's gap sentence below follows this: "not reported in the studies we reviewed".
research/journal/LESSONS.md:27:**L1.7 Every Introduction ends with a contribution list and a roadmap by section numbers.** 101498, 101106, 101216, 101470 all do; 101498's roadmap even lists what Section 4 contains (results, discussion and threats). Paper A: roadmap names Section 6 for Discussion with Limitations and threats.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:29:**L1.8 What none does and Paper A can: state the measured question in the first paragraph of the Introduction body.** All 16 introduce by context and literature. A single sentence stating the quantity measured (the share of the perfect-information saving a deployable scheduler realises, and what it loses to each input) gives the editor E2 ("the one finding to remember") early.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:31:### L1 proposed opening plan for Paper A (five paragraphs, about 700 to 850 words)
research/journal/LESSONS.md:33:2. What is known (3 to 4 sentences). Temporal shifting studies (Wiesner 2021, Sukprasert 2024, 101498, 101407, COAST, LiteCast) report savings with perfect or noise-perturbed signals and, mostly, known runtimes; 101470 scores an oracle gap by horizon for wind power, with unit tasks.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:38:**Gap sentence (respects A13 forbidden wording; no "first", no "no prior work", names the three outside works):**
research/journal/LESSONS.md:39:"Studies that vary forecast error inject synthetic noise or assume ideal job lengths, and studies on real HPC logs assume known runtimes [Wiesner 2021; Sukprasert 2024; 101498; LACS; COAST; LiteCast]; the saving a deployable scheduler realises with a trained past-only forecast and the user-requested walltime, and how the lost saving divides between those two inputs, is not reported in the studies we reviewed."
research/journal/LESSONS.md:40:Check against A13: cites the five SUSCOM papers' common assumptions without a "none of" claim extended to the field; LACS, COAST, LiteCast named (A13 point 4). Verify the three outside papers' wording from full texts before submission (A13 section 2: "not re-audited here").
research/journal/LESSONS.md:44:## L2 Contribution and novelty sentences
research/journal/LESSONS.md:46:**L2.1 SUSCOM lists are short and nominal.** A14 section 4.1: 3.5 items on average, T median 3, 9 of 10 nominal or passive voice. 101498: "A holistic formulation ... is proposed"; 101470: "A prediction-driven two-stage scheduling framework is proposed"; 101106: noun phrases ("Power, performance and energy theoretical models ..."). Paper A should use noun phrases, "we" optional.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:48:**L2.2 A measurement paper's contributions are measurements and a design, not a method.** No exemplar contributes a measurement-only study; they propose frameworks. Paper A must therefore say that no new scheduler is proposed (W8) and make the design (2 x 2 plus Shapley plus preregistration) the method contribution. A13 section 3 gives the defensible claims; use them.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:50:**L2.3 The release bullet has a precedent in the closest paper** (101498 bullet 3; correction above). Keep it as the last bullet.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:52:**L2.4 Contributions avoid numbers in 101498 and 101470 bullets but not in their abstracts.** Keep numbers out of the bullets (consistency with the ledger), put them in the abstract and Results.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:54:**L2.5 No exemplar claims "first" in the bullets.** 101498 hedges once in the gap sentence only. Paper A: nothing at all (W4; A13 wording to avoid).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:56:### L2 proposed contribution bullets (nominal voice; A13 defensible claims 1 to 4)
research/journal/LESSONS.md:57:- A pre-registered measurement, on real HPC batch-job traces and 42 US balancing authorities with a held-out year and block-bootstrap intervals, of the share of the perfect-information carbon saving that delay scheduling realises with a past-only forecast. (A13 claim 1)
research/journal/LESSONS.md:58:- A 2 x 2 design (signal by runtime estimate) with a Shapley allocation that divides the gap between the perfect-information and the deployable scheduler into a forecast term and a runtime term, and the saving recovered by a history-based runtime prediction. (A13 claims 2 and 3)
research/journal/LESSONS.md:59:- A comparison of the deployable scheduler with non-forecast baselines (run-now, night rule) and several forecasters under every runtime estimate, with small and negative results reported as found. (A13 claim 4)
research/journal/LESSONS.md:60:- The release of code, derived data, the result ledger, the pre-registration and its checksum. (101498 precedent)
research/journal/LESSONS.md:61:If the owner wants three bullets (A14 median), merge bullet 3 into bullet 1.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:65:## L3 Result reporting
research/journal/LESSONS.md:67:**L3.1 Numbers come with a "from a to b" sentence and then a mechanism sentence.** 101470 (md:L1195-L1210): "Relative to Oracle, the WU gap widens from 1.03 % to 7.38 % and 11.33 %", then "The widening Oracle gap reflects the limitation of prediction-based planning under increasing uncertainty." Paper A: after each estimate, one interpretive sentence, hedged only on the mechanism (W15).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:69:**L3.2 Results tables carry row groups: Proposed, Ideal (Oracle), Baseline, Ablation; spread as mean ± SD over seeds, "-" for deterministic rows with a note.** 101470 Tables 5, 8, 9 (A14 section 2). Paper A's main table should use the same row groups (Perfect-information, Deployable, Runtime variants, Baselines), with 95 % interval columns, not ± SD, and a note that all rows come from one simulation of the same jobs.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:71:**L3.3 Improvement over a named baseline in percent beside absolute means.** 101498 Tables 8 to 11 give improvement over the Local baseline. Paper A: report percentage points of relative saving against run-now (as defined in PR section 5) and an absolute quantity (g CO2 per kWh of job energy, or kg CO2 per MWh of job energy). The ledger has relative savings only; add a ledger key for the run-now mean intensity per trace (derived from the saved energy sums) so the absolute value is computed by script, never by hand. Referees flag relative-only reporting (BB B10).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:73:**L3.4 Statistics reporting that was criticised and why.** 101407 reports a 95 % CI over n = 3 task profiles and then rejects a null (md:L903-L914, A13): an interval over archetypes is not an interval over jobs. 101398 reports stars with no test named (A14 figure notes). Paper A: always state the unit (fixed 42 BAs, 7-day blocks within BA, B = 2000), the sidedness and the null-centring (PR section 6), and report "p < 0.0005" for the floor value (A08 check 3f; ledger key `TEST.PM100.w.S12.H1_deployable_saving.p_holm` is 1/2001 times 3).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:75:**L3.5 Show the oracle gap as a function of a driver.** 101470 reports the gap by horizon. Paper A has the Shapley terms by slack (ledger keys `TEST.PM100.w.S2` to `S24.shapley.*`) and the retained share by slack and job-length class; present both as the analogue of A13 adopt item 1.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:77:**L3.6 Report the three traces side by side and let disagreement stand.** Ledger: on PM100 the runtime term exceeds the forecast term (`TEST.PM100.w.S12.shapley.runtime` and `.forecast`), the same on KIT FH2 (`TEST.KIT_FH2.w.S12.shapley.runtime`, `.forecast`), but not on F-DATA (`TEST.FDATA.w.S12.shapley.forecast` above `.runtime`). A sentence like "the runtime term is larger on two of three traces" is correct; "runtime matters more than the forecast" is not. All H1 to H3 are supported on all three traces (Holm p keys `TEST.<trace>.w.S12.H1_deployable_saving.p_holm`, `H2_...`, `H3_...`).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:79:**L3.7 Retained share is the headline ratio.** `TEST.<trace>.w.S12.shapley.retained_share` is the deployable saving divided by the oracle saving with actual runtime (PM100, F-DATA, KIT FH2 differ; report each). Pair it with the absolute oracle saving, because a retained share of a small number is a small number (a referee will notice that the oracle saving itself is small on these grids: `TEST.PM100.w.S12.saving.oracle|act`).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:81:**L3.8 Small and negative effects go in the table, not only in prose.** The night rule saves nearly nothing on PM100 and loses on F-DATA (`TEST.PM100.w.S12.saving.night`, `TEST.FDATA.w.S12.saving.night`); persistence loses (A08 3f). 101470 reports Pred-Off and OnlyRL honestly against its own method; 101398 includes a test table. Keep every baseline in T4.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:83:**L3.9 State what the intervals do not cover.** A08 T1: intervals condition on the observed job sample; add S1 (user-cluster bootstrap) results beside, labelled exploratory. 101498 reports means and SD over six seeds and no intervals; Paper A should say in one sentence what the interval is a statement about.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:85:**L3.10 Do not over-read a single scenario.** 101407's 75.7 % is a single-case comparison dominated by spatial arbitrage (A13 A4). Paper A pools over 42 BAs and jobs; say so once, in the Discussion comparison paragraph.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:89:## L4 Discussion and implications
research/journal/LESSONS.md:91:**L4.1 Structure that works: one discussion subsection per scenario or question, then a trade-off subsection, then threats.** 101498 section 5 (5.1 and 5.2 scenarios, a trade-off subsection, 5.5 Threats to validity). 101407 5.3 "Comparative benchmarking and critical discussion". Paper A: 6.1 What the 2 x 2 says, 6.2 Implications, 6.3 Comparison with earlier work (why 12 h slack on one grid gives smaller savings than the spatial or wind-driven studies, A13 answer item 1), 6.4 Limitations and threats to validity, 6.5 Reproducibility.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:93:**L4.2 Implications are written as who can do what.** 101498 section 5.1: lightweight workloads "could serve as a first mover" for sustainability-aware orchestration; 101429 6.7 adds an industrial-deployment paragraph (supervisory layer above the existing building energy management system). Paper A implications (counterfactual replay, so "would" and "could", not "will"):
research/journal/LESSONS.md:94:- Operators: log requested and actual runtime, add a history-based runtime prediction to the delay planner (H3 recovery, keys `TEST.<trace>.w.S12.H3_prediction_recovery.est`), and prefer carbon-aware delay only on grids with a large daily range (Spearman descriptive, Figure 11 key in CAPTIONS.md).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:100:**L4.3 Limitations: four of the six 2026 articles have a dedicated subsection** (A14 section 4.2): 101398 5.4, 101498 5.5 "Threats to validity", 101470 5.6, 101429 6.7. Forms differ: 101470 is three sentences ("First ... Second ... Finally ..."); 101498 uses run-in bold labels (Simulation-based methodology, Generality, Input data quality, Embodied impacts, Regional resource equivalence, Spatial shifting constraints, Economic costs), each with the weakness, why it may still hold, and what future work would do (md:L2966-L3060). The 101498 form is the best fit for a referee reader: it is skimmable and always pairs a threat with a mitigation. Paper A: run-in labels, 400 to 600 words, one label per A08 item: Job sample and intervals (T1), Data vintage and publication delay (T2), Average versus marginal signal and factors (T3), Cross-system pairing (T4), Capacity and queueing (T5), Estimand and BA selection (T6, M1), Scheduler is a heuristic, oracle is relative (T7), Counts of cleaned hours and the TEST-year arrival gap (M2, M3).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:102:**L4.4 Admit the assumption that a prior paper also makes, and say what changes.** 101498 admits runtimes known; Paper A tests exactly that. In the Discussion, state that the 101498 assumption "runtimes could be estimated from previous runs" is the position the pred arm tests (A13 adopt item 5).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:104:**L4.5 State the average-versus-marginal convention in Methods and repeat it as a limit.** 101398 section 3.3.2 gives a model paragraph (average intensity, defined as total grid emissions over total generation; location-based accounting, GHG Protocol Scope 2) and then asserts the choice "affects absolute emission calculations but not relative comparisons between schedulers". Paper A must not copy that assertion: A08 T3 says the effect on marginal impact is unknown. Cite Sukprasert (average versus marginal signals) for the caveat and put sensitivity S3 beside it.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:106:**L4.6 Conclusion: results first, then what to do, then future work in one short paragraph.** 101498: reductions with ranges, then "may be more relevant in practice" for temporal shifting, then robustness and future work. 101216: sentences with figures. 101106 states its own limit ("limited to a single-server approach"). Paper A conclusion: three results with keys, one sentence of implication, one sentence on limits, future work (marginal signals, queue-aware simulation, a US trace).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:110:## L5 Figures
research/journal/LESSONS.md:112:**L5.1 Best figure 1: 101470 Fig. 7** (per-seed thin lines with a shaded band, same 3 x 3 structure, one colour per method across the paper). Adopt for Paper A Figure 8 (saving against slack): one thin line per alignment replicate (3) per arm plus the fixed-BA band, panels per trace. It shows whether replicate spread is smaller than the arm gaps.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:114:**L5.2 Best figure 2: 101216 Fig. 6** (mean with 95 % t-interval above, box plot below, whisker rule in the caption, lettered panels). Adopt for Figure 9 (per-BA forest): panel (a) sorted per-BA penalty with the full within-BA interval, panel (b) a box plot of the 42 per-BA values with the whisker rule in the caption.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:116:**L5.3 101470 Fig. 3 pattern: proposed method thick and solid, baselines dashed or grey, legend in the first panel only, off the data.** Paper A: the deployable arm drawn thick, perfect-information arms thin, baselines grey; one legend per figure outside the data.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:118:**L5.4 One colour per arm family held across all figures** (101470 and 101106 do it with non-safe palettes; A14 section 3). Encode the 2 x 2 with two channels: colour = runtime information (actual, requested, predicted), marker or line style = signal (open or dashed = oracle, filled or solid = LightGBM). Then every figure can show all four cells plus pred without a legend of more than 8 entries (A14 F12; 101429 Fig. 10 and 100950 Fig. 6 are the failures). Baselines grey with direct labels.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:120:**L5.5 Waterfall instead of stacked bars for the Shapley split.** A waterfall from perfect-information saving to deployable saving (steps: runtime term, forecast term; interaction shared as in PR 7.1) on a zero-based axis, one panel per trace, intervals on each step once the post-hoc script exists. This also shows the "runtime larger on two of three traces" fact without a stacked bar. Avoid: 3D bars (101429 Fig. 10), radar fills (101333 Figs 4 and 7), a line across categorical models (101333 Figs 3 and 6), truncated bar axes (101398 Fig. 8).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:122:**L5.6 Captions: finding plus n plus interval type, under 35 words.** Current CAPTIONS.md lengths are Fig 1 = 78 words, Figs 6, 7 = 50, Fig 14 = 52, Figs 8 to 10, 13 = 42 to 45 (my count); only Figs 11 and 12 are under 35. 101498 states findings in captions with numbers; keep that, trim the rest.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:124:**L5.7 Consistency between panels helps readers** (101106: same strip structure and colours across Figs 5 to 10). Use one panel structure (PM100, F-DATA, KIT FH2 in that order, same y-limits where comparable) in Figures 6, 7, 8, 10.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:126:**L5.8 Font and legend rules where peers fail:** tick text under 7 pt in 101498 Figs 5 and 11, 101333 Figs 5 and 8, 101106 Figs 5 to 10; labels overlapping error bars in 101398 Fig. 9. Keep F3 (at least 7 pt at print size) and check at print size.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:128:### Concrete changes proposed to `research/FIGURE_PLAN.md`
research/journal/LESSONS.md:130:- FP-2 Figures 11 and 12 merged into one two-panel figure (a) oracle saving against daily range, (b) runtime penalty against solar share, with the Spearman values in panels (they are already near the 35-word caption limit). Result: 13 figures, which keeps the owner's 13 to 14 rule while the typeset median stays about 9.5 (A14 section 4.1); if the owner prefers fewer, also fold Figure 13 (delay cost) into Figure 6 as panel (b), giving 12.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:131:- FP-3 Figure 6: dot plot on a zero-based axis with run-now at zero, perfect-information arms open markers, deployable arms filled; fixed-BA interval as a thick bar, two-stage as a thin whisker (already FIGURE_FIXES 1); add the best simple policy as a labelled grey marker; panels by trace.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:132:- FP-4 Figure 7: waterfall (L5.5), intervals added when the post-hoc script exists.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:133:- FP-5 Figure 8: replicate lines plus band (L5.1); add the S2 data-latency lines as a labelled exploratory panel only if S2 has run.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:134:- FP-6 Figure 9: two panels (L5.2).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:135:- FP-7 Figure 14: forest plot of D, H2 and H3 under each variant (primary, near-limit, unweighted, two-stage, DEV, S1 to S5); exploratory rows in a visually separate block with the label "post-freeze, exploratory"; replaces table T6's role as a figure, while T6 stays as the numeric grid.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:136:- FP-8 New optional Figure 15 slot: runtime-estimate sweep curve (L7 X1) if X1 runs; do not add it if the count would exceed 14.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:137:- FP-9 Figure 1: keep one panel for the 2 x 2 for a computing reader (F10); cut the caption to 35 words.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:139:### Concrete changes proposed to `figures/FIGURE_FIXES.md`
research/journal/LESSONS.md:140:- FF-1 Add item: Figure 1 and all captions to at most 35 words (current counts above); Figure 14's "move in step" check stays (item 4).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:149:## L6 Phrasing referees in this field expect
research/journal/LESSONS.md:151:**L6.1 Average versus marginal intensity.** Say "average (production-based) carbon intensity: lifecycle emissions of generation in the balancing authority divided by its generation, location-based (Scope 2) accounting" (101398 section 3.3.2 uses the same convention). Say "the saving is a saving in accounted average intensity", not "emissions avoided". Say "marginal intensity, the emissions of the next unit of demand, is the quantity relevant to a demand change and is not modelled" (101407 uses MER; 101498 uses average). Cite Sukprasert for the comparison. Never write that the choice does not matter for relative comparisons (L4.5).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:153:**L6.2 Slack.** Define once as "slack S: the maximum time a job's start may be delayed" and use "delay tolerance" or "flexibility window" only as synonyms in Related work (101498 uses "delay tolerance", dt 4 to 48 h; 101407 uses "slack time"). In tables and arms use S. Never use "deadline" for S unless the deadline is arrival plus slack plus runtime.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:155:**L6.3 Walltime.** "Requested walltime: the time limit the user supplies at submission, which the scheduler enforces (a job reaching it is killed, state TIMEOUT)". "Actual runtime: the measured run time." No SUSCOM paper uses either term (A14 section 4.3: 0 matches), so the definition belongs in the Introduction or Section 3, with the semantics audit (PM100 `time_limit`, F-DATA `elpl`, KIT FH2 status field; A08 4c) in the data section and the near-limit sensitivity named.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:157:**L6.4 Oracle and perfect information.** 101470 defines "Oracle. A perfect-information benchmark. It solves the same MILP with realised data" (md:L841-L845). Paper A: "perfect-information arm (oracle): the same planner given the realised intensity (and, in oracle|act, the actual runtime). It is not a global optimum of any scheduling problem" (A08 T7). Keep the label oracle|act in tables and "perfect information" in prose.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:165:**L6.8 Carbon-agnostic baseline = run-now.** 101407 "Local-Immediate", 101498 "Local". Use "run-now (start at arrival)" and "carbon-agnostic" once.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:167:**L6.9 Balancing authority (BA).** Define at first use: an entity that balances supply and demand on a section of the US grid and reports to EIA-930; do not call them "regions" or "grids" without saying a BA is meant.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:169:**L6.10 American English and "data center".** Confirmed in W14; 101498 mixes (analyzes, hyperscale). Keep American.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:173:**L6.12 No first-person excess:** 3 to 7 "we" per 1,000 words (A14 section 4.1; 101498 at 6.9 is the closest exemplar).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:177:## L7 Analyses the frozen design still allows (labelled exploratory), ranked
research/journal/LESSONS.md:179:Conditions for every item (from P4 and PR section 9): post-freeze, exploratory, new code in new files (src/sens_*.py importing frozen modules, none edited), grid fixed and md5-written before the first run, one row in `research/PREREG_DEVIATIONS.md`, own ledger `tables/ledger_TEST_sens_<id>.csv`, reported beside the frozen value whatever it shows, no promotion to confirmatory, nothing chosen on TEST (any noise or tuning scale is fitted on DEV). CPU only; the P4 resource gate applies (at least 6 GB free RAM, one heavy job at a time across papers). Items X1 to X7 go beyond P4 S1 to S6.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:183:| 1 | X1 | Runtime-estimate sweep | How accurate must a runtime estimate be to keep the saving? Where do requested walltime and the history prediction sit on that curve? | Planner estimate R_hat = R_act x m for m in {0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 8} (and a log-normal noise variant with the SD matched to the log of R_req over R_act measured on DEV), signals oracle and lgbm, S = 12, three traces; plot saving against estimate error with req and pred marked. Light: arms only, about the cost of the existing arms, memory as the frozen run | High: turns H2 and H3 into an operator rule; compares with LACS (synthetic length error) |
research/journal/LESSONS.md:184:| 2 | X2 | Injected noise versus real forecast | Does the noise-injection practice (101498 MAE 0.05 to 0.2; Wiesner 5 %; 101216 Fig. 11) over or understate the loss from a trained forecaster? | Signal = actual intensity x (1 + eps) with eps scale per lead chosen so the MAE matches LightGBM MAE at leads 1 to 24 on DEV (not TEST), arms noisy|act and noisy|req; compare saving with lgbm|act and lgbm|req. Light to moderate; no model fit | High: answers a methodological question the corpus leaves open |
research/journal/LESSONS.md:186:| 4 | X3 | Forecast-lead cap | How far ahead must the forecast reach? | Planner sees forecast leads up to L in {6, 12, 24, 48, 96}, holding the lead-L value beyond (as the frozen design does beyond 96); lgbm|req and lgbm|act, three traces. Forecast table already holds all leads: light | Medium to high: parallels 101470's horizon analysis |
research/journal/LESSONS.md:187:| 5 | X5 | Concentration of the runtime penalty | Is the H2 penalty driven by loosely estimated jobs, long jobs or a few users? Is it fixable by user education? | Stratify the existing job-level results by decile of R_act over R_req and by the top users (users counted in S1); share of penalty by stratum; per-BA unweighted mean and median beside the pooled estimate (A08 T6, option 3). Needs job-level output; if absent, rerun two arms with job dump. Light | Medium to high: gives the operator implication a number |
research/journal/LESSONS.md:192:Order of work when RAM allows: X1, X3 (light), X2, X4, X5, X6, then X7 after S3. None of X1 to X7 touches the frozen estimates, and each yields one figure panel or one row of T6, not new main figures (FP-8 aside).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:196:## L8 Title options (finding-led, no negatives, under 18 words, no acronym needed, no question mark)
research/journal/LESSONS.md:198:Facts to respect: the deployable arm keeps part of the perfect-information saving (`TEST.<trace>.w.S12.shapley.retained_share`, a share for each trace); the runtime term exceeds the forecast term on PM100 and KIT FH2 and not on F-DATA (`TEST.PM100.w.S12.shapley.runtime`/`.forecast`; `TEST.KIT_FH2...`; `TEST.FDATA.w.S12.shapley.forecast` larger; FIGURE_FIXES 2). Therefore no title says that runtime matters more than the forecast. Numbers stay out of titles (0 of 16 SUSCOM titles have them; ledger consistency). Word counts are mine.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:200:1. "Forecast and walltime information jointly determine the carbon saving of delay-scheduled HPC batch jobs" (14 words). Finding-led; true for all three traces (both terms are positive; interaction small on all three traces: `TEST.<trace>.w.S12.shapley.interaction` is about a tenth of `.gap` or less); owner-style. Untested form in SUSCOM (0 of 16 finding-led).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:201:2. "Carbon savings of delay-based HPC batch scheduling with past-only forecasts and requested walltimes" (13 words). Verb-less descriptive, the 101498 pattern (verb-led or descriptive, no colon); safest for scope and editor.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:202:3. "Carbon-aware delay scheduling of HPC batch jobs: how much perfect-information saving a deployable scheduler realizes" (15 words). Descriptive object plus a "how much" clause, W1's recommended shape; the clause names the measured quantity without a number.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:203:4. "Decomposing the gap between ideal and deployable carbon-aware HPC job scheduling into forecast and runtime terms" (16 words). Method-first (the 2 x 2 and Shapley design is the contribution); "gap" is mildly negative, so use only if the owner accepts it.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:204:5. "A pre-registered evaluation of deployable carbon-aware delay scheduling for HPC batch jobs across US grids" (15 words). Highlights the rigour differentiator (0 of 10 topic-close papers pre-register) but has a trailing region phrase the owner dislikes (MP s8).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:205:6. "Carbon savings that HPC batch jobs realize when delayed using past-only forecasts and requested walltimes" (15 words). Finding-oriented phrasing, plain verbs, no acronym beyond HPC; close to option 2 with the subject as the jobs.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:207:Recommendation: option 1 if the owner keeps the finding-led rule, option 2 as the fallback that matches the SUSCOM norm (A14: median 14 words, 0 of 16 finding-led, so option 1 carries editor-reaction risk G4). Define HPC in the abstract. The owner approves the final title.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:211:## Lesson index (49)
research/journal/LESSONS.md:214:## Checks
research/journal/LESSONS.md:216:- A13 forbidden wording: "first", "no prior work", water, land, spatial, wind claims, "significant" for non-Holm, comparison with 20 to 85 %: all avoided in proposed text.
research/journal/LESSONS.md:217:- Quotes: 101498 "To the best of our knowledge" is not reproduced as a quote; the others are paraphrases except the 101470 definition phrase (6 words) and the "rarely exploit" fragment (2 words).
research/journal/LESSONS.md:218:- Not done: reading the 101216 introduction in full and 101333; both gave no extra lesson for Paper A beyond A14.
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:1:VERDICT: brief v1 (A17), 88 rules, 8 open gaps
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:5:v1 written 2026-11-12 by agent A17 from v0 (A11; kept as `JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF_v0.md`), `journal/A14_STYLE_MEASURES.md` (16 papers measured), `journal/A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md` (novelty wording) and `journal/LESSONS.md` (L1 to L8). A change log at the end lists every edit. Short names: JB = `_shared/JOURNAL_BASICS.md`; JP = `_shared/JOURNAL_PROFILE_SUSCOM.md` (P.n = its "Paywalled pass" items, which win over earlier sections where they differ); JPA = `paper_A/research/JOURNAL_PROFILE_A.md`; SP = `STYLE_PROFILE.md`; BB = `BEST_PAPER_BAR.md`; FP = `FIGURE_PLAN.md`; MP = `_shared/MEGA_PROMPT.md`; PR = `PREREG.md`; PB = `paper_B/research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md`; A13, A14, LS = the three files named above. Numbers: only ledger keys from `tables/ledger_TEST.csv` (and `ledger_TEST_*.csv`, `ledger_DEV*.csv`); never type a result. Facts not in the files are marked "not established".
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:7:## 1. The paper in five lines
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:11:4. Finding: see ledger keys `TEST.PM100.w.S12.H1_deployable_saving.*`, `...H2_runtime_penalty.*`, `...H3_prediction_recovery.*`, and the Shapley keys `TEST.PM100.w.S12.shapley`. State each with the PR s8 wording; never quote a number from this brief.
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:14:## 2. Journal rules (source in brackets)
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:16:R2. Word or page limit: none stated in the guide. Plan 9,000 to 11,000 words counted from the title to the References heading (captions and table cells included; prose alone is about 8,000 to 10,000), about 14 to 19 typeset pages, 9 to 12 figures and 6 to 9 tables; say which counting basis a quoted number uses. Do not pad above 11,000. [JB s3; A14 s4.4 change 1: median 11.1k words and 16 pages, 2026 pages 9, 16, 18, 19, 19, 32; the prose-versus-total split is A17's reading]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:17:R3. Abstract: at most 250 words, stand-alone, no references, abbreviations defined; working range 200 to 230 words, one paragraph. [JB s3; JP P.2 item 2, P.5 item 1] Count on the Word render. (STYLE_PROFILE and BB say 220 to 250; the controller's update supersedes: stay at or below 230.) Note: 5 of 15 typeset abstracts are at or above 250 words (101398 275, 100592 265, 101216 256, 100985 251, 101470 250), so the limit is not a safety margin and is not enforced at typeset stage; keep Paper A at 200 to 230. [A14 s4.4 change 2]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:18:R4. Highlights: required at submission; 3 to 5 bullets; at most 85 characters each including spaces; separate editable file with "highlights" in the file name; do not embed in the manuscript body. [JB s3; JP P.2 item 14, P.5 item 6]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:19:R5. Keywords: 1 to 7, English, avoid multi-word keywords containing "and" or "of"; aim at 5 to 6. [JB s3; JP P.2 item 4]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:20:R6. Graphical abstract: optional; separate file at least 1328 px wide, readable at 13 cm. Decision on whether to make one: open (not established that it helps).
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:23:R9. References: numbered in square brackets in order of appearance; include DOIs; mark preprints "preprint"; cite datasets with [dataset] and software as references; journal names per LTWA at proof. Target 40 to 55 (median 36 to 42, range 21 to 78). Every DOI a blue clickable link (owner rule, D0012). [JB s3; JP P.2 item 9, P.5 item 2; progress.md D0012] Note: SUSCOM house style prints DOIs in only 6 of 16 reference lists, so DOIs are an owner-rule addition, not a journal requirement; target 40 to 55 (median 49, topic-close 40.5) stands. [A14 change 3]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:24:R10. Declarations: competing-interest file from the Elsevier declarations tool (separate upload); funding sentence (exact no-funding wording in JB s4, `[USER]` flag until the owner says otherwise); CRediT (14 roles) after the Conclusion and before References. [JB s4; MP s12]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:25:R11. Generative-AI declaration: ONLY in its own section before the reference list, with the Elsevier template title and sentence verbatim (JB s4). Never in Methods, Acknowledgements, cover letter or title page. Scope wording: confirm with the owner; never change a statement the owner or professor has edited. [JB s4; MP s12; owner rule, D0012] Note: none of the 16 typeset papers (six from 2026) shows an AI-declaration section or title line, so the wording cannot be checked against a published example; use the Elsevier template wording from JB s4 and keep the section because the owner rule requires it. [A14 change 4]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:27:R13. Code availability: private GitHub; the owner gives the link before any push; wording uses a placeholder until then. [D0012]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:28:R14. Figures: separate files, vector PDF with embedded fonts plus 300 dpi PNG, colour-blind-safe, one image per file; no AI manipulation of primary images. Tables: editable text, notes below, no vertical rules or shading. [JB s3]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:29:R15. Peer review: single anonymized, at least two reviewers; editors may desk-reject; first-decision time 1 day (publisher insights page; JIF year not verified). [JB s1]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:32:## 3. Style rules for writers
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:34:W1. Title: owner wants a finding-led title with no negatives and no trailing region or dataset phrase (MP s8). SUSCOM evidence: 14 of 16 titles have at most 16 words; 0 of 16 are finding-led; 9 of 16 use a colon (5 with a method name); descriptive, verb-led and colon-subtitle forms all occur. Rule: at most 16 words, descriptive object or finding clause, no question mark, no acronym beyond HPC (define in the abstract), no method-name colon. A finding clause is allowed but untested in this journal (editor reaction not established, G4); the fallback is a verb-less descriptive title of the 101498 type. Candidates with rationale: LS L8 (six options). Titles must not say that the runtime term exceeds the forecast term in general: it does on PM100 and KIT FH2 and not on F-DATA (figures/FIGURE_FIXES item 2; ledger shapley keys). The owner approves the final title; it stays provisional until all analyses are done (MP s8). [A14 change 5; LS L8]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:35:Abstract (200 to 230 words, one paragraph, 6 to 9 sentences)
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:36:W2. Moves in order: (1) sustainability context in one sentence, with the computing lever; (2) gap in one sentence (deployable saving is rarely scored with real forecasts and real runtime estimates); (3) design with data named (42 BAs, three job traces, held-out year, pre-registered); (4) two to four results with interval each (H1, H2, H3 and the Shapley split); (5) one honest weak or null result; (6) one implication sentence for operators. [JP P.2 item 3; SP finding 4; JPA s5] Three to five numbers; every one a ledger key. Define abbreviations at first use in the abstract (BA, HPC, EIA).
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:38:W3. Introduction plan (LS L1): paragraph 1 states the sustainability stake in one cited figure and the computing lever; paragraph 2 maps temporal-shifting studies in one sentence with bracketed ranges and states what they assume (perfect or noise-perturbed signals, runtimes known: 101498, Wiesner 2021, Sukprasert 2024; COAST, LiteCast, LACS named); paragraph 3 says what a deployed scheduler sees (a past-only forecast and the requested walltime) and carries the gap sentence; paragraph 4 gives the design in two sentences; then 3 to 4 contributions in nominal or passive voice (3 is the median, range 2 to 5; "we" optional), the last one the release of code, derived data, ledger, pre-registration and checksum (101498 ends its list with a release item; 101106 releases code and data), then a roadmap sentence by section number that names Section 6 as Discussion with limitations. A one-paragraph definition of run-now, slack, requested walltime and the perfect-information arm sits in the Introduction (101407 does the same for its terms). Gap sentence and bullets: LS L1 and L2. [A14 change 6 as corrected by LS (101498 does end with a release bullet); A13 s3; LS L1, L2]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:39:W4. Contribution verbs: "we quantify", "we measure", "we decompose", "we release". Banned: "novel", "first", "to the best of our knowledge", "groundbreaking". [PB s2 rule 4; JP s14 shows these as common weakness in the corpus] Also forbidden (A13 s3): "first to compare forecast-based with perfect-information carbon-aware scheduling", "first to show forecast error erodes saving", "first study on real traces and many grids", "no prior work treats runtime uncertainty" (LACS models synthetic length error), any claim about water, land use, spatial shifting or wind, and a numeric comparison to the headline figures of 101498, 101216, 101470, 101407 or 100950. 101498 uses the hedge "to the best of our knowledge"; Paper A does not; it writes "we found no study that ..." or "is not reported in the studies we reviewed". [A13 s3; LS L1.3]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:41:W5. Order: 1 Introduction; 2 Related work (with comparison table); 3 Data; 4 Method (arms, estimands, scheduler, forecasting, inference); 5 Results (RQ-by-RQ or H1-H3, then secondaries); 6 Discussion (trade-offs, delay cost, BA character, Threats to validity, Reproducibility); 7 Conclusion; then CRediT, declarations, AI declaration section, data and code availability, references. A separate Related work section appears in 13 of 14 typeset papers. [JP P.2 items 5, 12; JPA s5]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:43:W6. One comparison table (T1 in FP). Rows: Wiesner 2021, Sukprasert 2024, Radovanovic 2023, CarbonCast 2022 (stronger venues); SUSCOM 101498, 101216, 101470, 101407, 100950; LACS, COAST and LiteCast (outside works that bound the runtime-error and real-trace claims, NOVELTY_SCAN s.b); Paper A. Columns (A13 s4, cells read in full for the five SUSCOM papers): decision, workload, carbon or renewable signal, forecast, oracle, runtime uncertainty, evaluation scale, statistics, code. Fill cells only from what was read; "not extracted" for Wiesner, Sukprasert and Radovanovic runtime cells; "not re-audited" for LACS, COAST, LiteCast until read. Use the A13 s4 draft and the A13 s5 related-work sentences (2 to 4 per paper). [A13 s4-s5; BB B9]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:44:W7. Positioning claims allowed (A13 s3, a measurement at scale, not a first): (1) a measurement of the share of the perfect-information saving a deployable scheduler realises on real HPC traces with a past-only forecaster on 42 US BAs and a pre-registered held-out year; (2) the saving lost when the planner uses the requested walltime, and the saving a history prediction recovers; (3) a 2 x 2 design with a Shapley allocation of the gap into forecast and runtime terms; (4) results beside non-forecast baselines and several forecasters under every runtime estimate with negative findings stated. Say that the forecast-to-oracle gap is known: 101470 reports it by horizon for wind power with unit tasks and no runtime (1.03 % to 21.88 % in its Table 8), Wiesner and Sukprasert use error scenarios; Paper A's value is a real past-only forecaster, real jobs and a second information factor. "None of the five SUSCOM papers" is a statement about those five only; extend nowhere without LACS, COAST and LiteCast named. [A13 s2-s3]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:47:W9. Limitations and threats to validity: heading "Limitations and threats to validity" (precedent: Limitations three times, Threats to validity once; 4 of 6 articles in 2026 have one), placed in or directly after Discussion. Form: run-in bold labels, each paragraph one weakness, why the result may still hold, what would test it (101498 section 5.5 pattern; LS L4.3), 400 to 600 words; the label-per-paragraph form and length are Paper A's choice. One label per item: job sample and interval scope (A08 T1; S1); data vintage and EIA publication delay (T2; S2); average versus marginal signal, unverified oil, other and unknown factors, storage and imports excluded (T3, 4a, 4b; S3); cross-system pairing of foreign traces on US grids (T4; S6); no queueing, capacity, PUE or interruptibility (T5); estimand and BA selection on TEST availability (T6, M1); delay-to-lowest-integral heuristic and a relative oracle (T7); KIT FH2 1 kW and status limits, unscored forecaster tuning until S5 is done, cleaned-hour counts and the TEST-year arrival gap (M2, M3, M7, D0012). [A14 change 7; A08 s2-s3; LS L4.3]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:48:W10. Reproducibility subsection: one-command rerun, frozen md5s, ledger, Zenodo placeholder (101106 has a dedicated heading). [JPA s2.2] A dedicated Reproducibility subsection occurs in 1 of 15 typeset papers (101106 5.1) and a one-line reproducibility sentence in three more, so it is a differentiator, not a conformity requirement. [A14 G8]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:49:W11. Preregistration disclosure: state the freeze date, md5, each deviation (PREREG_DEVIATIONS.md) and that the P4 items S1 to S5 are post-freeze and exploratory, labelled as such in text, tables and captions. [PR s9; P4_PLAN]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:51:W12. "We" for actions and decisions (Methods steps, design choices); passive for set-up and data description; 3 to 7 "we" per 1,000 words (median 3.4, topic-close 3.75, 8 or more in 2 of 15; 101498 at 6.9 is the closest exemplar); at most 30 % passive (not re-measured, A14 s5). [A14 change 8; JP s14; SP finding 2]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:52:W13. Sentences: mean 22 to 25 words, none above 40, hard cap 45; one claim per sentence. [SP finding 1; MP s9]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:53:W14. No em or en dashes (use commas, colons, "to"). One English variant: American (measured in the SUSCOM paper and all four stronger-venue texts); e.g. "data center". [JP s14; SP finding 6]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:54:W15. Hedging about 1 per 100 words, on mechanisms and interpretations only, never on measured numbers. [SP finding 3]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:56:W17. Every claim about practice (EIA publication delay, HPC user behaviour with walltimes, grid mixes) carries a citation or is softened. [MP s11]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:57:W18. Show absolute (kgCO2 or g per job-hour) and relative effects with intervals; relative-only reporting is a flagged weakness. [BB B10]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:58:W19. Describe each result with the PR s8 wording; secondary and post-freeze items are never called confirmatory; H4 is descriptive (demoted on Codex R03).
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:59:W20. No disparaging wording about prior work; negative and null results stated plainly. [CLAUDE.md rule 6]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:60:W21. Define each abbreviation again at first use in the body; keep arm labels exactly: oracle|act, oracle|req, lgbm|act, lgbm|req (D), lgbm|pred, lgbm|pred2, run-now, night, persistence, seasonal naive day and week, profile7, histprofile. [FP; PR s4]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:61:W22. Definitions, in the Introduction or Section 3 (LS L6): average (production-based, location-based Scope 2) carbon intensity as the accounted signal, marginal intensity named as the decision-relevant signal that is not modelled; slack S (maximum start delay; "delay tolerance" and "flexibility window" only as synonyms in Related work); requested walltime (the limit the user supplies, enforced by killing the job, state TIMEOUT) versus actual runtime; perfect-information arm and "oracle" (the same planner given the realised intensity, not a global optimum); deployable (uses only what is known at submission); balancing authority (BA) at first use; run-now as the carbon-agnostic baseline. Never write that the average-versus-marginal choice leaves relative comparisons unchanged (101398 does; A08 T3 says the effect is unknown); cite Sukprasert for the comparison and point to S3. [LS L6; A08 T3; 101398 s3.3.2]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:62:W23. Units and wording of effects: saving is relative (1 minus emissions over run-now emissions); differences of savings are percentage points (pp); penalty and recovery are pp; retained share is a ratio (`shapley.retained_share`, deployable over perfect-information saving); "significant" only for the Holm-adjusted bootstrap result of H1 to H3; report p at the bootstrap floor as "p < 0.0005"; one interpretive (mechanism) sentence after each estimate, hedged on the mechanism only. [LS L3.1, L3.4, L6.6, L6.7; A08 3f]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:63:W24. Result reporting (LS L3): the main table has row groups (perfect-information, deployable, runtime variants, baselines) like 101470 Tables 5, 8, 9; every headline estimate carries a 95 % interval with its type, an absolute effect (g CO2 per kWh of job energy, from a ledger key to be added: run-now mean intensity per trace, A17 gap G12) and the relative effect; all three traces side by side; small and negative baselines (night rule, persistence) stay in the table; one sentence says what the interval is a statement about (the fixed 42 BAs, conditional on the observed job sample; S1 beside it, labelled exploratory). Retained share is paired with the absolute oracle saving. [LS L3.1-L3.9; A14 s4.5]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:64:W25. Per-trace sentences must follow the ledger: the runtime term exceeds the forecast term on PM100 and KIT FH2 and not on F-DATA (keys `TEST.<trace>.w.S12.shapley.runtime` and `.forecast`); H1 to H3 are confirmatory on PM100 only (PREREG s6) and supported there (Holm p keys); on F-DATA and KIT FH2 the same contrasts are secondary replications and are described as such, never as confirmed (controller fix 04:40, flagged by A18); write "on two of three traces", never "runtime matters more than the forecast" without the trace. [LS L3.6; figures/FIGURE_FIXES item 2]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:65:W26. Discussion structure (LS L4.1-L4.2): 6.1 what the 2 x 2 shows; 6.2 implications for operators (log requested and actual runtime, add a history-based predictor), users (walltime accuracy has a carbon value; a limit set too low kills the job), scheduler designers (forecast and runtime are separate levers; the planner is the delay-to-lowest-integral policy, no scheduler is ranked) and signal providers (data latency); 6.3 comparison with earlier work (why 12 h slack on a fixed grid gives smaller savings than the spatial or wind-driven studies; the single-case 101407 headline versus pooled 42 BAs); 6.4 limitations and threats (W9); 6.5 reproducibility (W10). Implications are counterfactual: "would" and "could", never "will be realised". [LS L4; A13 s6; EDITOR R05 q4]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:66:W27. Adopt from earlier work (A13 s6) and say so in text: (1) report the gap and retained share by slack, parallel to 101470's gap by horizon; (2) name oracle|req as the walltime-only cost, parallel to the forecast-only gap of 101470; (3) an injected-error sweep (101216 Fig. 11) only as exploratory X2 (LS L7), never confirmatory; (4) keep run-now and night rule in T4 beside the deployable arm; (5) cite 101498's "runtimes could come from previous runs" as the position the pred arm tests. Answer in Discussion: 101216's zero-error effect has no analogue because the planner is one-stage; code and data release (101470, 101216, 101407, 100950 do not release) mentioned once. [A13 s6]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:68:## 4. Figure and table rules
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:69:F1. Count: plan 9 to 12 figures and 6 to 9 tables (topic-close median 9.5 figures and 7.5 tables; overall 11 and 9; the ceiling is set by new information: 101429 has 41 figures with the same plot repeated for modes A, B and C). The project rule is 13 to 14 figures (FP has 14): decision for the owner (G11). Recommendation: 13, by merging FP 11 and 12 into one two-panel figure; 12 if FP 13 is also folded into Figure 6. Keep each figure only if it carries new information. [A14 change 9; LS L5; CLAUDE.md MP s7]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:71:F3. Widths 90, 140 or 190 mm; text at least 7 pt at print size; 300 dpi PNG plus vector PDF with embedded fonts; one image per file. [MP s7; JB s3]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:73:F5. Uncertainty visible: 95 % intervals on every headline estimate. State of the field: 5 of 10 topic-close papers show some spread (101216 t-interval in a figure; 101398 ± SD and a Bonferroni test table; 101407 a CI over n = 3; 101470 ± SD over five seeds; 101429 Monte Carlo n = 30), 0 of 10 use a bootstrap, a pre-registered interval type or a hypothesis structure, and 1 of 10 reports p-values (101398). Paper A is ahead on interval type and pre-registration, not on showing a spread at all. [A14 change 10]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:74:F6. Captions: a short title clause, one sentence stating the finding, then n (BAs, jobs) and the interval type, then "Exploratory, post-freeze" where applicable; figure captions at most 35 words and table captions at most 35 words (topic-close figure captions already run 18 to 35 words, up to 91 in 101106; table captions median 34; two papers state findings with numbers in captions: 101498, 101398). Current `figures/CAPTIONS.md` exceeds the cap in 8 of 10 captions (Figure 1 has 78 words; A17 count). [A14 change 11; LS L5.6]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:77:F9. Main result table (T4): one row per estimand H1 to H4; columns estimate, 95 % interval and interval type (fixed-BA stratified block bootstrap), raw p, Holm p, decision, n BAs, plus the same on F-DATA and KIT FH2 in a second block; baselines beside it (run-now, night, histprofile, persistence, seasonal naive, oracle). Best value in bold, consistent decimals. [PR s6; JP P.2 item 7; BB B4-B5] Layout precedents: 101470 (row groups Proposed / Ideal (Oracle) / Baseline / Ablation, mean ± spread, "-" for deterministic rows with a note) and 101398 (best value bold, a test table); Paper A replaces ± SD with 95 % intervals and Holm-adjusted p. [A14 s4.5; LS L3.2]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:78:F10. Figure 1 is the schematic of the 2x2 design for a computing reader, written so that no carbon-grid jargon is needed to follow it. [FP 1]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:79:F11. Algorithm boxes appear in 8 of 15 typeset papers (topic-close 5 of 10, range 0 to 3): include one (the pre-registered simulation and bootstrap procedure) and more only where a component is introduced. Numbered equations: topic-close median 12, range 0 to 51; plan 12 to 25, numbered and referenced. [A14 change 12]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:80:F12. Failures seen in the 16 papers, forbidden here: 3D bars (101429 Fig. 10); radar charts with translucent fills (101333 Figs 4, 7); a line drawn across categorical models (101333 Figs 3, 6); truncated bar axes (101398 Fig. 8, y axis from 75); value labels on top of error bars (101398 Fig. 9); infographic equivalents (101398 Fig. 15); a legend with more than 8 entries or dash codes that carry meaning (100950 Fig. 6); tick text under 7 pt at print size (101498 Figs 5, 11; 101333 Figs 5, 8; 101106 Figs 5 to 10); the same plot repeated for each mode (101429); significance stars with no named test (101398). Savings axes start at zero with a thin run-now line. [A14 change 13]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:81:F13. Encoding and designs to adopt (LS L5): the 2 x 2 is encoded with two channels, colour for runtime information (actual, requested, predicted) and marker or line style for signal (open or dashed for oracle, filled or solid for LightGBM), held fixed in every figure, baselines grey with direct labels, one legend outside the data; Figure 8 follows 101470 Fig. 7 (thin line per alignment replicate plus the interval band); Figure 9 follows 101216 Fig. 6 (interval plot above, box plot below, whisker rule in the caption); Figure 7 is a waterfall from the perfect-information saving to the deployable saving on a zero-based axis; one panel structure (PM100, F-DATA, KIT FH2) in every multi-trace figure (101106 consistency). [A14 s3; LS L5.1-L5.7]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:82:F14. Post-freeze exploratory results (S1 to S5, X1 to X7) appear in figures and tables only in a visibly separate block labelled "post-freeze, exploratory", beside the frozen value, never in the main confirmatory panel. [PR s9; P4; LS L7]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:84:## 5. Personas for reviewer agents
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:85:### 5a. SUSCOM handling editor (decision within about a day)
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:86:Checks first: scope (computing for or of sustainability), importance of the finding, clarity for a computing reader, declarations complete.
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:87:Likely rejection reasons: reads as energy-systems rather than computing (the owner's earlier desk rejection elsewhere was scope only, JP s16 item 6); incremental scheduling benchmark; missing declarations or empty data statement; overclaim.
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:93:| E4 | Which SUSCOM papers does it cite and position against? | Related work, T1 (101498, 101216, 101470, 101407, 100950) |
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:95:| E6 | Abstract at most 250 words; highlights 3 to 5 at most 85 characters; keywords at most 7? | R3-R5; Word render count |
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:97:| E8 | Any "novel", "first", unsupported practice claim? | W4, W17 |
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:98:| E9 | Does the title, abstract and Introduction avoid "first" and unsupported "none"? | W4; A13 s3 wording list |
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:99:### 5b. Domain referee: carbon-aware computing and HPC scheduling
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:100:Checks first: realism of the job model and signal; whether the baseline ladder includes the simple policies that Sukprasert finds capture most gain; average versus marginal intensity; whether results depend on one trace.
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:101:Likely rejection reasons: average intensity answers a marginal question; delay-only, no interruptibility, no capacity limit; US grids with Japanese, Italian and German traces; requested-walltime semantics (censoring at the limit, killed jobs) not audited; improvement reported relative to run-now only; EIA publication delay ignored. Attacks added from A13 and NOVELTY_SCAN: (i) "ReBase (101470) already scores a trained forecaster against a perfect-information Oracle by horizon" (true; it uses wind power, unit tasks, a fixed carbon factor, no runtime, one information factor); (ii) "LACS models job-length error, COAST uses an HPC trace with known runtimes, LiteCast runs a real forecast on the KIT-FH2 log with ideal job lengths"; (iii) "101498 already has real traces and four of your grids" (synthetic arrivals, one week per season, noise as forecast, no oracle, runtimes assumed known); (iv) "EuroSys 2024 and LiteCast already show forecast error costs little"; (v) "a 12 h slack gives far smaller savings than the 20 to 85 % in the spatial literature"; (vi) "a CI over three archetypes (101407) is what you replace with a bootstrap: is yours over jobs?".
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:109:| D6 | What does the saving cost in delay? | FP 13 delay-cost figure |
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:112:| D9 | Is ReBase's oracle gap not the same result? | Intro gap sentence; T1 row 101470; W7: known gap by horizon for wind and unit tasks, no runtime term; Paper A adds a second information factor and real jobs |
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:115:| D12 | Do relative comparisons survive a marginal signal? | W22 (not claimed); S3 and X7 labelled exploratory; threats label on signal |
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:116:| D13 | Is runtime knowledge a realistic input? | Pred arm: previous runs of the same user, causal assertion in `src/prep_jobs.py`; H3; X1 curve (exploratory) |
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:117:### 5c. Statistics and reproducibility referee
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:118:Checks first: unit of inference, preregistration and freeze, multiplicity, dependence handling, leakage.
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:119:Likely rejection reasons: intervals that ignore job-sampling variance (A08 threat; S1); inference target unclear (fixed 42 BAs versus a population); post-freeze analyses presented as confirmatory; one-sided p with a null-centred bootstrap not explained.
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:122:| S1 | Unit of inference and target? | PR s5-6: the fixed set of 42 BAs; two-stage bootstrap shown beside as a population reading |
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:126:| S5 | Are the intervals complete (alignment replicates, job sample)? | S1 sensitivity; disclose if not run |
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:129:| S8 | Is the interval a statement about the job sample? | Method and Limitations (W24): conditional on the observed job sample; S1 labelled exploratory |
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:131:### 5d. Desk-reject screen (run before every submission candidate)
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:132:- [ ] Title at most 16 words, no "first", no claim that runtime beats the forecast in general (W1, W25).
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:133:- [ ] Abstract at most 250 words (target 200 to 230); sentence 1 states the sustainability outcome; numbers match the ledger.
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:134:- [ ] Intro cites and positions against 101498, 101216, 101470, 101407, 100950 and the stronger venues.
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:135:- [ ] Highlights 3 to 5, each at most 85 characters; keywords 1 to 7.
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:138:- [ ] No "novel", "first", unsupported practice claims; no em or en dashes; American English.
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:139:- [ ] Limitations and threats subsection present with run-in labels (W9); definitions paragraph present (W22); T1 has the A13 columns (W6).
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:140:- [ ] Figures: two-channel encoding, zero-based savings axes, captions at most 35 words, none of the F12 failures.
