paper/SPEC.md:1:VERDICT: A18 done; SPEC v0; 8 sections; 234 numbers registered; 30 open items
paper/SPEC.md:5:Binding inputs: `research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md` v1 (rules R1-R16, W1-W27, F1-F14, personas 5a-5d; cited below as R, W, F), `research/journal/LESSONS.md` (L1-L8), `A13_OVERLAP_AUDIT.md` (A13), `A15_RELATED_WORK.md` and `A15_REFERENCES.csv` (A15), `research/PREREG.md` (PR, frozen 2026-11-11, md5 `cfe984017ef7c790d851bfa2f519fde3` per `PREREG_DEVIATIONS.md`), `PREREG_DEVIATIONS.md` (DEV rows 1-4), `P4_PLAN.md` v2 (S1-S6) and `P4_PLAN_X.md` (X1-X7), `FIGURE_PLAN.md` (FP), `figures/CAPTIONS.md`, `figures/FIGURE_FIXES.md` (FF), `A05`-`A09`, and the four ledgers. Numbers: every value in section 7 was read from the ledger or table named there on 2026-11-12; none is typed from memory. This file is a blueprint for section writers and the expert panel. It contains no manuscript prose beyond the plans, drafts marked DRAFT, and the wording the brief fixes.
paper/SPEC.md:7:How writers cite numbers: write a macro such as `\num{TEST.PM100.w.S12.H1_deployable_saving.est}` or a text token `{{key}}`; a build script resolves it from the ledger. A key missing from the ledger is a build error (W16). Rounding rule proposed here (owner to accept): savings and percentage-point (pp) differences to one decimal in prose, two decimals in tables; p-values at the bootstrap floor written "p < 0.0005"; shares to two significant figures. Section 7 gives the unrounded value as stored.
paper/SPEC.md:9:Unit and label conventions fixed for all writers: arm labels exactly as W21 (oracle|act, oracle|req, lgbm|act, lgbm|req (D), lgbm|pred, lgbm|pred2, run-now, night, persistence, seasonal naive day and week, profile7, histprofile); PM100, F-DATA, KIT FH2 always in that order; "perfect-information arm (oracle)" in prose; "requested walltime" and "actual runtime"; saving relative to run-now; differences in pp; American English; no em or en dashes; no "novel", "first", "to the best of our knowledge" (W4); one `\papertitle` macro for the title.
paper/SPEC.md:13:## 1. Working title (OWNER TO APPROVE; the text uses the macro `\papertitle`)
paper/SPEC.md:15:From LESSONS L8 (word counts by A17); both are at most 16 words (W1), neither contains a number, a question mark or a method-name colon, and neither says that runtime matters more than the forecast in general (W25):
paper/SPEC.md:17:1. OWNER TO APPROVE: "Forecast and walltime information jointly determine the carbon saving of delay-scheduled HPC batch jobs" (14 words). Finding-led, true on all three traces (both Shapley terms are positive and the interaction is small: keys `TEST.<trace>.w.S12.shapley.forecast`, `.runtime`, `.interaction`). Untested form in SUSCOM: 0 of 16 titles are finding-led (A14; risk G4). "HPC" is defined in the abstract.
paper/SPEC.md:18:2. OWNER TO APPROVE (fallback that matches the SUSCOM norm): "Carbon savings of delay-based HPC batch scheduling with past-only forecasts and requested walltimes" (13 words).
paper/SPEC.md:22:## 2. The story and the three claims
paper/SPEC.md:26:| # | Claim the paper makes | Ledger keys (PM100 first; F-DATA and KIT FH2 by the same pattern) | Strength |
paper/SPEC.md:28:| C1 | A deployable scheduler (past-only LightGBM forecast, requested walltime, arm D = lgbm\|req) realises a positive saving that is a minority to a half of the perfect-information saving at S = 12 h. | `TEST.PM100.w.S12.H1_deployable_saving.est/.lo/.hi/.p_holm` (N016 est with interval, N017 Holm p); `TEST.PM100.w.S12.saving.oracle\|act`; `TEST.PM100.w.S12.shapley.retained_share`; the same keys for FDATA, KIT_FH2 | H1 is confirmatory on PM100 only (PR s6). On F-DATA and KIT FH2 the same test is secondary (PR s7.6); the F-DATA and KIT FH2 H1 numbers are reported as secondary replications, never as confirmations. |
paper/SPEC.md:29:| C2 | Planning with the requested walltime instead of the actual runtime costs saving (H2), and a history-based runtime prediction recovers part of it (H3). | `TEST.PM100.w.S12.H2_runtime_penalty.est/.lo/.hi/.p_holm`; `TEST.PM100.w.S12.H3_prediction_recovery.est/.lo/.hi/.p_holm`; `TEST.PM100.w.S12.perba_H2.pos/.sig/.n`; two-stage intervals `TEST.PM100.w.twostage.S12.H2_runtime_penalty.lo/.hi` | H2 and H3 confirmatory on PM100 (Holm over H1-H3); the other traces secondary. Per-BA counts and two-stage intervals secondary (PR s7.3, s7.12). |
paper/SPEC.md:30:| C3 | The gap between perfect information and the deployable scheduler splits into a forecast term and a runtime term; the runtime term is larger on PM100 and KIT FH2, the forecast term on F-DATA. | `TEST.<trace>.w.S12.shapley.gap/.forecast/.runtime/.interaction/.retained_share`; `H4_interaction.est/.lo/.hi` | Secondary and descriptive (PR s7.1, s7.9; H4 demoted on Codex R03). Shapley intervals need a post-hoc script (FF item 2); until it exists the terms are point estimates. |
paper/SPEC.md:32:| (pending) | Data latency, tuned forecaster, factor choice, user-cluster uncertainty, runtime-estimate accuracy curve, injected-noise comparison, load concentration. | `sens.S1`-`sens.S5` and `sens.X1`-`sens.X7` ledgers (not yet produced) | EXPLORATORY, post-freeze, pending P4. Never in the main confirmatory panel (F14). |
paper/SPEC.md:34:## 3. Abstract plan (target 200 to 230 words, hard limit 250, one paragraph, 6 to 9 sentences; R3, W2)
paper/SPEC.md:36:Moves in order, with word budget and the numbers each carries (key in brackets; rounded values as of today, PM100 unless stated):
paper/SPEC.md:45:| 6 Implication | 25 | Operators logging requested and actual runtimes and adding a history predictor would recover part of the lost saving; counterfactual wording only (W26). | none |
paper/SPEC.md:47:Abbreviations to define in the abstract: HPC, BA, EIA (written "U.S. Energy Information Administration (EIA)"), pp (or write "percentage points"); define again in the body at first use (W21). The abstract has no reference, no "first", no "novel". Count words on the Word render (R3). Do not call the F-DATA and KIT FH2 H1-H3 results "confirmed".
paper/SPEC.md:49:## 4. Highlights draft (3 to 5 bullets, each at most 85 characters including spaces; separate file; R4)
paper/SPEC.md:53:1. "Deployable delay scheduling keeps one third of the perfect-information saving" [`TEST.PM100.w.S12.shapley.retained_share`, N031]
paper/SPEC.md:55:3. "Runtime term exceeds the forecast term on two of three job traces" [`TEST.<trace>.w.S12.shapley.runtime`, `.forecast`; N029, N028, N082, N081, N135, N134]
paper/SPEC.md:59:## 5. Section-by-section outline (total target 9,000 to 11,000 words title to References, counting captions and table cells; prose alone about 8,500; R2)
paper/SPEC.md:61:Word budget: Abstract 220; 1 Introduction 1,100; 2 Related work 1,150; 3 Data 950; 4 Method 2,100; 5 Results 2,600; 6 Discussion 1,550; 7 Conclusion 300; 8 Declarations 250; captions and table text about 1,300 (inside the total). The sum is about 11,500 with captions; writers cut the Discussion and Related work first if the Word render exceeds 11,000 (R2: do not pad above 11,000). "ref:" lists A15 keys; "NEW" marks a reference that is not yet in A15 (section 8). Equations are numbered in order of appearance; the list below is the master list (E1-E19).
paper/SPEC.md:63:### 1 Introduction (1,100 words; about 5 paragraphs; W3, L1, L2)
paper/SPEC.md:67:2. What is known (3 to 4 sentences): temporal-shifting studies in one sentence with bracketed ranges; what they assume (perfect or noise-perturbed signals, known runtimes). ref: wiesner2021, sukprasert2024limits, radovanovic2023, suscom_101498, suscom_101407, suscom_101216, suscom_101470, lacs2024 (NEW), coast2026 (NEW), litecast2025 (NEW).
paper/SPEC.md:68:3. What a deployed scheduler sees (3 sentences): a past-only forecast and the requested walltime, a loose upper bound. Numbers: median actual-to-requested ratio per trace (M-rows for the median ratio, `tables/jobs_trace_stats.json`; needs a ledger key, open item 14). ref: tsafrir2007, mualem2001 (NOT quoted for figures until read, open item 11).
paper/SPEC.md:69:4. Gap and design (2 + 2 sentences). Gap sentence is LESSONS L1, verbatim shape: "Studies that vary forecast error inject synthetic noise or assume ideal job lengths, and studies on real HPC logs assume known runtimes [...]; the saving a deployable scheduler realises with a trained past-only forecast and the user-requested walltime, and how the lost saving divides between those two inputs, is not reported in the studies we reviewed." Do not write "first", "no prior work", or "none of the five" for the field (A13 s3). State that the forecast-to-oracle gap itself is known (101470 by horizon for wind power and unit tasks; Wiesner and Sukprasert with error scenarios).
paper/SPEC.md:73:Figures: Fig 1 (study design schematic, first cited at the end of paragraph 4). Tables: none. Equations: none. Algorithms: none. Ledger keys: none (no result numbers in the Introduction except the cited ratio in point 3).
paper/SPEC.md:75:### 2 Related work (1,150 words; W6, W7, A13 s4-s5)
paper/SPEC.md:76:Purpose: place the study against the closest work and give the comparison table; wording from A13 s5 (2 to 4 sentences per paper, factual).
paper/SPEC.md:77:2.1 Temporal shifting and carbon-aware computing (350 words): Wiesner 2021 (delay tolerance, forecast errors of 5 % injected, optimal arm), Sukprasert 2024 (123 regions, simple versus sophisticated policies, prediction-error scenario), Radovanovic 2023 (production), CarbonCast (forecast, about 10 % error, not a savings study), hanafy2024green, hanafy2023carbonscaler, souza2023casper, cucumber2022, lechowicz2025, hall2025, asadov2025. ref list: wiesner2021, sukprasert2024limits, radovanovic2023, maji2022carboncast, hanafy2024green, hanafy2023carbonscaler, souza2023casper, linchien2023, cucumber2022, lechowicz2025, hall2025, asadov2025.
paper/SPEC.md:78:2.2 SUSCOM papers (450 words, the A13 s5 sentences): 101498 (Attenni; real Azure and Spark traces with synthetic arrivals, noise-perturbed mix as forecast, runtimes assumed known), 101216 and 101470 (Zhai; wind power, aggregate load or unit tasks; 101470 reports the forecast-to-oracle gap by horizon, 1.03 % to 21.88 % in its Table 8, so the gap is known), 101407 (Liow; n = 3 profiles, CI over archetypes), 100950 (Chen; static factors, offline). Also 100989 (review), 101429, 101106, 100592 as one clause each. Say "None of the five SUSCOM papers" only as a statement about those five (W7). ref: suscom_101498, suscom_101216, suscom_101470, suscom_101407, suscom_100950, suscom_100989, suscom_101398, suscom_101429, suscom_101106, suscom_100592.
paper/SPEC.md:79:2.3 Runtime estimates and prediction in HPC (200 words): requested walltime is an upper bound; history-based prediction (Tsafrir 2007, Gaussier 2015, Fan and Lan 2017, Mu'alem and Feitelson 2001, Klusacek 2024); no inaccuracy figure is quoted until the papers are read (open item 11). Outside carbon works that bound the runtime-error and real-trace claims: LACS (synthetic job-length error), COAST (HPC trace, known runtimes, actual carbon), LiteCast (real forecast on the KIT-FH2 log, ideal job lengths), all "to read" (G10; open item 10). ref: tsafrir2007, mualem2001, gaussier2015, fan2017, klusacek2024, feitelson2004, lacs2024 (NEW), coast2026 (NEW), litecast2025 (NEW).
paper/SPEC.md:80:2.4 Position (150 words): the four allowed claims (W7), the admitted known gap, and "the planner is the delay-to-lowest-integral policy of Wiesner and Sukprasert" (W8).
paper/SPEC.md:81:Tables: T1 comparison table (rows: Wiesner 2021, Sukprasert 2024, Radovanovic 2023, 101498, 101216, 101470, 101407, 100950, LACS, COAST, LiteCast, Paper A; columns: decision, workload, carbon or renewable signal, forecast, oracle, runtime uncertainty, evaluation scale, statistics, code; A13 s4 cells; "not extracted" for the Wiesner, Sukprasert and Radovanovic runtime cells; "to read" for LACS, COAST, LiteCast). Figures: none. Equations, algorithms: none. Ledger keys: none (headline figures of other papers are not compared with ours, A13 s3).
paper/SPEC.md:83:### 3 Data (950 words)
paper/SPEC.md:85:3.1 Grid carbon intensity (350 words): EIA-930 hourly generation by fuel for the BAs; production-based average intensity with IPCC AR5 lifecycle medians (`src/emission_factors.csv`; oil, other and unknown are documented judgement values, 6 of 9 factors verified: A06); cleaning rules and flag counts; eligibility rule (first valid hour on or before 2018-09-01, last valid hour on or after 2026-06-30, at least 90 % valid hours in each of TRAIN, DEV and TEST, mean generation at least 100 MW, TRAIN standard deviation above 1 g/kWh) giving 42 of 70 BAs; EIA publication delay assumption (grid data for the hour ending floor(a) is available at time a) stated here and again in 6.4; accounting convention (W22). ref: miller2020, tranberg2019, leerbeck2020, siler2012, sukprasert2024avgmarg, koebrich2025, schlomer2014, NEW: EIA-930 [dataset], NEW: GHG Protocol Scope 2.
paper/SPEC.md:86:3.2 Job traces (400 words): PM100 (Marconi100, primary, CC-BY-4.0), F-DATA (Fugaku, five months, CC-BY-4.0), KIT FH2 (Parallel Workloads Archive; no power, 1 kW per job so energy weight is proportional to runtime; every SWF status is COMPLETED so TIMEOUT cannot be identified; licence terms to be checked, open item 8); job filter (COMPLETED, runtime at least 1 min, requested walltime at most 72 h and above 0); power treated as whole-job mean, never multiplied by nodes; walltime semantics audit (PM100 TIMEOUT jobs sit within 2 % of the limit; median actual-to-requested ratios by trace); NLR Kestrel not obtained (HTTP 503; open item 29), stated plainly. Fig 3 (ECDF of actual over requested runtime per trace, inset job-length classes). ref: antici2023pm100, antici2024fdata, feitelson2014pwa, klusacek2024.
paper/SPEC.md:87:3.3 Periods and what-if pairing (200 words): TRAIN 2018-07-01 to 2024-06-30, DEV 2024-07-01 to 2025-06-30, TEST 2025-07-01 to 2026-06-30, scored once after the freeze; arrivals fall between 2025-07-07 and 2026-06-14 (DEV row 4); jobs from 2016, 2020 and 2021 to 2023 are replayed on 2025-26 US grids at their local weekday and clock time (the what-if wording of L6.11; T4). Fig 2 (the 42 BAs: median daily intensity range against mean intensity, size = generation, colour = solar share).
paper/SPEC.md:88:Tables: T2 (BAs, traces, periods; rows: traces with jobs in, completed, users, median ratio; BA eligibility counts; periods). Equations: none. Algorithms: none.
paper/SPEC.md:89:Ledger and table keys: M-rows (candidate and eligible BAs, rows in, completed rows, median ratios, TIMEOUT counts, flag shares, users); `TEST.PM100.w.n_ba`. All M-rows need ledger keys before use (open item 15).
paper/SPEC.md:91:### 4 Method (2,100 words)
paper/SPEC.md:93:4.1 Planner and realised emissions (300 words): job with arrival a, requested walltime R_req, actual runtime R_act, mean power P; slack S in {2, 6, 12, 24} h (primary 12 h); candidate starts on a 0.25 h grid; the planner minimises the integral of its signal over [s, s + R_hat] and ties go to the earliest start; the planner sees 98 hourly intervals from floor(a); forecast leads 1 to 96, with intervals 96 and 97 holding the lead-96 value; jobs whose window touches an unusable hour are excluded; realised emissions are scored on the actual intensity over [s, s + R_act]. Equations E1-E4. Algorithm 1 (Plan). ref: wiesner2021, sukprasert2024limits.
paper/SPEC.md:94:4.2 Arms and the 2 x 2 design (250 words): signals oracle, persistence, seasonal naive day, seasonal naive week, profile7, LightGBM, historical profile; runtime estimates act, req, pred, pred2; D = lgbm|req (selected on DEV before TEST); non-forecast baselines run-now and night rule; Fig 1b (the 2 x 2). Table T3 (arms and estimands).
paper/SPEC.md:95:4.3 Forecasters (300 words): global LightGBM with horizon as a feature, trained on TRAIN only, never refit; causal inputs (flag 1 and 2 hours forward-filled at most 6 h); future-perturbation test: 150 tests, 0 failed; DEV skill (pooled 1 to 24 h MAE lgbm against seasonal naive day) and BA counts (38 of 42, 41 of 42) as the basis for choosing D; frozen hyperparameters without tuning (HPO sensitivity S5, exploratory). Equation E5 (forecast issue and lead holding). ref: friedman2001 (reserve), NEW: LightGBM, maji2022carboncast, hyndman2006, cerqueira2020.
paper/SPEC.md:96:4.4 Runtime estimates (200 words): pred = mean actual runtime of the user's two most recent jobs that ended before the job's submission with state COMPLETED or TIMEOUT, capped at R_req; R_req if no history; pred2 = min(R_req, 2 x pred), k = 2 fixed before TEST; leakage assertion in `src/prep_jobs.py` (1,000 jobs recomputed naively, passed). Equation E6. Algorithm 2 (Runtime estimate). ref: tsafrir2007, gaussier2015, fan2017.
paper/SPEC.md:97:4.5 Job sampling and calendar alignment (200 words): each job is placed in a random week of the period with its own local weekday and clock time in the BA's standard local time; 3 alignment replicates by 20,000 jobs per BA per trace (all jobs if fewer); seed 20261111; unweighted outcome is the per-job mean realised intensity (DEV row 1). Algorithm 3 (Calendar alignment). Equation E7.
paper/SPEC.md:98:4.6 Estimands and the Shapley split (300 words): saving(arm), pooled over BAs, days and replicates, energy-weighted; inference target is the fixed set of 42 eligible BAs over the TEST year (selected on coverage, not sampled); H1, H2, H3; retained share; Shapley allocation of the gap with the interaction shared equally; H4 descriptive. Equations E8-E13. ref: lundberg2020, owen2014.
paper/SPEC.md:99:4.7 Inference (300 words): stratified block bootstrap with all 42 BAs kept in every replicate; 7-day blocks of arrival days resampled within BA; B = 2000; seed 7 + S; one-sided null-centred p; Holm over H1-H3 at alpha = 0.05; 95 % percentile interval for every estimate; two-stage bootstrap (BAs then blocks) shown beside as a reading for a hypothetical BA population, not a second confirmation; what the interval is a statement about (fixed BAs, conditional on the observed job sample; W24). Equations E14-E16. Algorithm 4 (Evaluation and bootstrap). ref: efron1979, politis1994, NEW: Holm 1979.
paper/SPEC.md:100:4.8 Pre-registration, deviations and exploratory analyses (250 words): freeze date 2026-11-11 IST, md5, code md5s at freeze (PR header); deviations DEV rows 1 to 4; secondary analyses were computed by post-freeze code `src/secondary.py` that only reads saved results (DEV row 3); the post-freeze exploratory items S1 to S5 and X1 to X7 are named here with one sentence each, with the rule that they are reported beside the frozen values and never promoted (W11, F14). Algorithm 5 (final: end-to-end study procedure, calls Algorithms 1 to 4). ref: nosek2018.
paper/SPEC.md:101:Figures: Fig 1 (second citation), Fig 4 (worked example: one BA, one week, actual and forecast intensity at an arrival, start chosen under act and req). Tables: T3. Ledger keys: PR constants only (B, block length, replicates, seed, jobs per BA); no result numbers except the DEV skill counts (M-rows).
paper/SPEC.md:103:### 5 Results (2,600 words; W19, W23-W25)
paper/SPEC.md:104:Order follows the figures; each estimate is followed by one mechanism sentence (L3.1). All three traces side by side; per-trace sentences follow W25. Each subsection says "confirmatory" or "secondary" in its first sentence.
paper/SPEC.md:105:5.1 Forecast skill on TEST (250 words): median-over-BA MAE by lead bucket for lgbm, seasonal naive day, profile7, persistence, seasonal naive week; LightGBM lower than seasonal naive day in 37 of 42 BAs on TEST; confound caveat for the Spearman of MAE with the forecast term (headroom). Fig 5. Keys: M-rows (forecast MAE table), N197 (Spearman forecastgap). Secondary (PR s7.7).
paper/SPEC.md:106:5.2 Main result, S = 12 h (confirmatory on PM100; 450 words): H1 est, interval, Holm p; the perfect-information saving beside it; run-now, night, simple-policy ladder in T4; two-stage interval beside the fixed-BA interval; F-DATA and KIT FH2 as secondary replications. Fig 6, T4. Keys: N016, N017 (H1), N004-N010 (arms), N003 and N011-N015 (baselines), two-stage N018; F-DATA at +53 and KIT FH2 at +106 on the same offsets. p reported as "p < 0.0005" at the floor (W23); Holm values are keys `p_holm`.
paper/SPEC.md:107:5.3 Runtime estimate and prediction (350 words): H2 and H3 with intervals; best deployable arm is lgbm|pred on every trace and slack (keys `best_deployable_arm_is_lgbm_pred`, `best_deployable_minus_D`); pred2 beside pred. Keys: N019-N024 (H2, H3, two-stage), N008-N010, N043, N044 (best deployable arm). T4, T5.
paper/SPEC.md:108:5.4 Decomposition (300 words): Shapley gap, forecast and runtime terms, interaction (H4 descriptive); the runtime term larger on PM100 and KIT FH2 and not on F-DATA; wording exactly per W25. Fig 7 (waterfall, point estimates unless the interval script exists, FF 2). Keys: N027-N031 (Shapley, PM100), N025, N026 (H4); +53 and +106 for F-DATA and KIT FH2.
paper/SPEC.md:109:5.5 Slack (250 words): saving against slack at 2, 6, 12, 24 h for the four cells plus pred; gap and retained share by slack (adopt item 1, A13 s6; analogue of 101470's gap by horizon); Shapley terms by slack. Fig 8 (thin line per alignment replicate plus the interval band, F13). Keys: N035-N040 (saving at S = 2, 6, 24 h, PM100) with +53 and +106, N160-N168 (PM100 Shapley by slack).
paper/SPEC.md:110:5.6 Per-BA heterogeneity (200 words): H2 positive in 41 of 42 BAs and the within-BA lower bound above zero in 41 on PM100; per-trace counts (F-DATA 40 and 33, KIT FH2 41 and 40); the BA coded SPA has a constant intensity and zero oracle saving, so its retained share is undefined (A09). Fig 9 (interval plot above, box plot below, whisker rule in the caption; needs the upper bound, FF 3). Keys: N032-N034 (`perba_H2.n/.pos/.sig`, PM100), +53, +106.
paper/SPEC.md:111:5.7 Job length and BA character (300 words): retained share by job-length class (descriptive, no interval); Spearman across the 42 BAs of the oracle saving with the daily intensity range and of the runtime penalty with solar share (TEST-hours characters; descriptive, unadjusted, many ties for solar and wind). Fig 10, Fig 11 and Fig 12 (merge decision G11). Keys: N041, N042 (pooled retained share), N169-N182 (classes), N189-N196 (Spearman).
paper/SPEC.md:112:5.8 Delay cost (150 words): mean start delay by arm; night rule 4.4 h for 0.3 % at S12; deployable arm about as long as the oracle. Fig 13. Keys: N045 (lgbm|req delay), N183-N188 (PM100 delay arms and slacks).
paper/SPEC.md:113:5.9 Robustness and secondary traces (350 words): near-limit exclusion, unweighted outcome, DEV against TEST, two-stage intervals, per trace; ordering holds in all 24 trace x slack x variant cells (A09 s6); the post-freeze items S1 to S5 and X1 to X7 in a visibly separate block "post-freeze, exploratory" beside the frozen value, only the ones that have run (F14). Fig 14, T5, T6. Keys: N046-N053 (near-limit, unweighted, DEV; +53, +106); `sens.*` keys when they exist.
paper/SPEC.md:114:Equations: E17-E19 used for reporting (retained share, delay, Spearman). Algorithms: none.
paper/SPEC.md:116:### 6 Discussion (1,550 words; W26, W27, L4)
paper/SPEC.md:117:6.1 What the 2 x 2 shows (300 words): the walltime-only cost is the oracle|req cell, parallel to the forecast-only gap of 101470; the runtime term versus forecast term by trace; the forecast term grows with slack; the interaction is small. Wording per W25.
paper/SPEC.md:118:6.2 Implications (300 words, counterfactual "would", "could"): operators (log requested and actual runtime, add a history-based predictor), users (walltime accuracy has a carbon value; a limit set too low kills the job, so the implication is for estimates, not shorter limits), scheduler designers (forecast and runtime are separate levers; no scheduler is ranked), signal providers (data latency, S2). Absolute effect reported beside the relative one (W18; key pending, open item 13). Never "will be realised".
paper/SPEC.md:119:6.3 Comparison with earlier work (250 words): why 12 h slack on a fixed grid gives smaller savings than the spatial or wind-driven studies, with no numeric comparison to their headline figures (W4); single-case 101407 headline against the pooled 42 BAs; 101216's zero-error effect has no analogue because the planner is one-stage; 101498's "runtimes could be estimated from previous runs" is the position the pred arm tests; code and data release mentioned once (101470, 101216, 101407, 100950 do not release).
paper/SPEC.md:120:6.4 Limitations and threats to validity (500 words; run-in bold labels, one weakness per paragraph with why the result may still hold and what would test it; W9, L4.3): Job sample and interval scope (T1; S1 exploratory beside); Data vintage and EIA publication delay (T2; S2); Average versus marginal signal, unverified oil, other and unknown factors, storage and imports excluded (T3, 4a, 4b; S3, X7 labelled); Cross-system pairing of foreign traces on US grids (T4; S6 text); No queueing, capacity, PUE or interruptibility (T5; X4 proxy); Estimand and BA selection on TEST availability (T6, M1); Delay-to-lowest-integral heuristic and a relative oracle (T7); KIT FH2 1 kW and status limits, frozen forecaster not tuned until S5, cleaned-hour counts and the TEST-year arrival gap (M2, M3, M7). Reference: sukprasert2024avgmarg, siler2012, hawkes2010, callaway2018, koebrich2025 for the marginal point.
paper/SPEC.md:121:6.5 Reproducibility (200 words): one-command rerun, frozen md5s, ledger, SHA256SUMS, Zenodo placeholder, private GitHub placeholder (R12, R13, W10).
paper/SPEC.md:122:Figures: none new. Tables: none new. Equations: none. Ledger keys: those of 5.2 to 5.5 re-quoted (identical values, W16).
paper/SPEC.md:124:### 7 Conclusion (300 words)
paper/SPEC.md:125:Results first (C1 to C3 with the keys of section 2), one sentence of implication (counterfactual), one sentence on limits, future work in one short paragraph (marginal signals, queue-aware simulation, a US trace such as NLR Kestrel when obtained, tuned forecasters if S5 does not close it). No new numbers.
paper/SPEC.md:127:### 8 Declarations (250 words; each its own heading, in this order after the Conclusion and before References; R10-R13, R16)
paper/SPEC.md:128:CRediT author statement (14 roles, placeholders for names); Declaration of competing interest (placeholder; the declarations-tool file is uploaded separately); Funding (exact no-funding sentence of JB s4 with `[USER]` flag until the owner says otherwise); Declaration of generative AI and AI-assisted technologies in the manuscript preparation process (the Elsevier template title, own section only, scope wording from the owner's open question 3; never in Methods, Acknowledgements, cover letter or title page); Data availability (Option C: Zenodo DOI placeholder; EIA-930 public domain, PM100 and F-DATA CC-BY-4.0, KIT FH2 licence to check); Code availability (private GitHub placeholder, link from the owner before any push); Acknowledgements (directly before References; placeholder); author names, affiliations, emails `[USER]`.
paper/SPEC.md:130:## 5b. Equations (master list, LaTeX, numbered in order of appearance; target 12 to 25; F11)
paper/SPEC.md:151:- E13 (retained share) `\rho=\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|req})/\sigma(\mathrm{oracle|act})`
paper/SPEC.md:153:- E14 (percentile interval) `\bigl[\,T^{*}_{(0.025B)},\ T^{*}_{(0.975B)}\bigr]`, B = 2000
paper/SPEC.md:159:- E19 (absolute effect; PENDING the key of open item 13) `\Delta_{\mathrm{abs}}=\sigma\cdot\bar c_{\mathrm{run\text{-}now}}`, with `\bar c_{\mathrm{run\text{-}now}}=\sum_j E_j^{\mathrm{run\text{-}now}}\big/\bigl(\sum_j P_jR_{\mathrm{act},j}\bigr)` in g CO2 per kWh of job energy
paper/SPEC.md:160:Exploratory (only if the items run): X1 `\hat R=mR_{\mathrm{act}}` for m in {0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 8}; S2 `\hat c_a` built from data up to `\lfloor a\rfloor-k`.
paper/SPEC.md:162:## 5c. Algorithms (algorithm2e style, numbered lines, vertical scope bars; one small algorithm per component, building to one final algorithm)
paper/SPEC.md:164:- Algorithm 1, Plan (4.1): Input a, S, \hat R, signal \hat c_a (98 intervals), Delta. Output s*. Steps: for each s in S(a,S) compute the integral over [s, s+\hat R] from the interval array; keep the minimum, earliest on ties; return s*. Function header `Plan`.
paper/SPEC.md:165:- Algorithm 2, RuntimeEstimate (4.4): Input job j, mode in {act, req, pred, pred2}, user history. Output \hat R. Steps: act returns R_act; req returns R_req; pred takes the user's last two jobs ended before the submission with state COMPLETED or TIMEOUT, averages, caps at R_req, falls back to R_req; pred2 applies min(R_req, 2 pred). Assertion on end < submit.
paper/SPEC.md:166:- Algorithm 3, AlignAndSample (4.5): Input job pool, BA, period, replicate index, seed. Output placed jobs with arrival a on the BA clock. Steps: draw min(20,000, pool size) jobs; draw a random week of the period; keep the job's local weekday and clock time in the BA's standard local time; drop jobs whose 98 h window touches an unusable hour.
paper/SPEC.md:167:- Algorithm 4, EvaluateAndBootstrap (4.7): Input per-BA daily sums of arm emissions, B, block length 7, seed 7 + S. Output estimates, 95 % intervals, null-centred p, Holm p. Steps: compute T on the data; for b = 1..B resample 7-day blocks within each BA (all 42 BAs kept), recompute T*_b; compute interval and p; apply Holm over H1 to H3.
paper/SPEC.md:168:- Algorithm 5, Study (4.8; final): Input traces, EIA-930 intensity, frozen PR. Output ledger. Steps: build and clean intensity; fit forecasters on TRAIN; select D on DEV; freeze (md5); for each trace, BA, slack, replicate call AlignAndSample; for each arm call Plan with RuntimeEstimate; score emissions; call EvaluateAndBootstrap; write ledger; run secondary analyses and label post-freeze exploratory items separately.
paper/SPEC.md:169:Count: 5 boxes; brief F11 says algorithm boxes occur in 8 of 15 typeset papers (topic-close range 0 to 3) and "more only where a component is introduced"; the owner rule (CLAUDE.md s9) asks for several; panel decides whether Algorithms 1 and 2 merge (open item 27).
