### 6 Discussion (1,550 words; W26, W27, L4)
6.1 What the 2 x 2 shows (300 words): the walltime-only cost is the oracle|req cell, parallel to the forecast-only gap of 101470; the runtime term versus forecast term by trace; the forecast term grows with slack; the interaction is small. Wording per W25.
6.2 Implications (300 words, counterfactual "would", "could"): operators (log requested and actual runtime, add a history-based predictor), users (walltime accuracy has a carbon value; a limit set too low kills the job, so the implication is for estimates, not shorter limits), scheduler designers (forecast and runtime are separate levers; no scheduler is ranked), signal providers (data latency, S2). Absolute effect reported beside the relative one (W18; key pending, open item 13). Never "will be realised".
6.3 Comparison with earlier work (250 words): why 12 h slack on a fixed grid gives smaller savings than the spatial or wind-driven studies, with no numeric comparison to their headline figures (W4); single-case 101407 headline against the pooled 42 BAs; 101216's zero-error effect has no analogue because the planner is one-stage; 101498's "runtimes could be estimated from previous runs" is the position the pred arm tests; code and data release mentioned once (101470, 101216, 101407, 100950 do not release).
6.4 Limitations and threats to validity (500 words; run-in bold labels, one weakness per paragraph with why the result may still hold and what would test it; W9, L4.3): Job sample and interval scope (T1; S1 exploratory beside); Data vintage and EIA publication delay (T2; S2); Average versus marginal signal, unverified oil, other and unknown factors, storage and imports excluded (T3, 4a, 4b; S3, X7 labelled); Cross-system pairing of foreign traces on US grids (T4; S6 text); No queueing, capacity, PUE or interruptibility (T5; X4 proxy); Estimand and BA selection on TEST availability (T6, M1); Delay-to-lowest-integral heuristic and a relative oracle (T7); KIT FH2 1 kW and status limits, frozen forecaster not tuned until S5, cleaned-hour counts and the TEST-year arrival gap (M2, M3, M7). Reference: sukprasert2024avgmarg, siler2012, hawkes2010, callaway2018, koebrich2025 for the marginal point.
6.5 Reproducibility (200 words): one-command rerun, frozen md5s, ledger, SHA256SUMS, Zenodo placeholder, private GitHub placeholder (R12, R13, W10).
Figures: none new. Tables: none new. Equations: none. Ledger keys: those of 5.2 to 5.5 re-quoted (identical values, W16).

### 7 Conclusion (300 words)
Results first (C1 to C3 with the keys of section 2), one sentence of implication (counterfactual), one sentence on limits, future work in one short paragraph (marginal signals, queue-aware simulation, a US trace such as NLR Kestrel when obtained, tuned forecasters if S5 does not close it). No new numbers.

### 8 Declarations (250 words; each its own heading, in this order after the Conclusion and before References; R10-R13, R16)
CRediT author statement (14 roles, placeholders for names); Declaration of competing interest (placeholder; the declarations-tool file is uploaded separately); Funding (exact no-funding sentence of JB s4 with `[USER]` flag until the owner says otherwise); Declaration of generative AI and AI-assisted technologies in the manuscript preparation process (the Elsevier template title, own section only, scope wording from the owner's open question 3; never in Methods, Acknowledgements, cover letter or title page); Data availability (Option C: Zenodo DOI placeholder; EIA-930 public domain, PM100 and F-DATA CC-BY-4.0, KIT FH2 licence to check); Code availability (private GitHub placeholder, link from the owner before any push); Acknowledgements (directly before References; placeholder); author names, affiliations, emails `[USER]`.

## 5b. Equations (master list, LaTeX, numbered in order of appearance; target 12 to 25; F11)

Notation: job j with arrival a, requested walltime R_req, actual runtime R_act, mean power P; slack S; start grid step Delta = 0.25 h; c(t) realised hourly intensity (piecewise constant); \hat c_a(t) the signal the planner sees at arrival a; \hat R the runtime the planner believes.

Section 4.1
- E1 (candidate starts) `\mathcal{S}(a,S)=\{a,\,a+\Delta,\,a+2\Delta,\dots,a+S\}`
- E2 (planner) `s^{\star}=\min\Bigl(\arg\min_{s\in\mathcal{S}(a,S)}\int_{s}^{s+\hat R}\hat c_{a}(t)\,\mathrm{d}t\Bigr)` (the outer min takes the earliest start on ties)
- E3 (realised emissions) `E=\frac{P}{1000}\int_{s^{\star}}^{s^{\star}+R_{\mathrm{act}}}c(t)\,\mathrm{d}t` (kg CO2 when c is in g/kWh and P in W)
- E4 (window) `\operatorname{frac}(a)+S+R_{\mathrm{req}} \le \operatorname{frac}(a)+24+72<98` (every job's plan and realisation fit inside the 98 intervals)
Section 4.3
- E5 (forecast lead holding) `\hat c_{a}(k)=\begin{cases}f_{\theta}(x_{\le\lfloor a\rfloor},\,k+1)&k=0,\dots,95\\ \hat c_{a}(95)&k=96,97\end{cases}` with f the forecaster trained on TRAIN only and x the causal inputs
Section 4.4
- E6 (runtime estimates) `\hat R_{\mathrm{pred}}=\min\bigl(R_{\mathrm{req}},\ \tfrac12(R_{(1)}+R_{(2)})\bigr)`, with `R_{(1)},R_{(2)}` the actual runtimes of the user's two most recent jobs that ended before the submission with state COMPLETED or TIMEOUT (`\hat R_{\mathrm{pred}}=R_{\mathrm{req}}` if none; one job gives its runtime); `\hat R_{\mathrm{pred2}}=\min(R_{\mathrm{req}},\,2\hat R_{\mathrm{pred}})`
Section 4.5
- E7 (unweighted outcome) `\bar c_{\mathrm{u}}=\frac1n\sum_{j}\frac{1}{R_{\mathrm{act},j}}\int_{s_j^{\star}}^{s_j^{\star}+R_{\mathrm{act},j}}c(t)\,\mathrm{d}t` (each job one weight; DEV row 1)
Section 4.6
- E8 (saving) `\sigma(\mathrm{arm})=1-\dfrac{\sum_{j}E_{j}^{\mathrm{arm}}}{\sum_{j}E_{j}^{\mathrm{run\text{-}now}}}`
- E9 (hypotheses) `\theta_1=\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|req}),\quad \theta_2=\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|act})-\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|req}),\quad \theta_3=\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|pred})-\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|req})`
- E10 (gap) `G=\sigma(\mathrm{oracle|act})-\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|req})`
- E11 (Shapley terms) `\phi_{F}=\tfrac12\bigl[\sigma(o|a)-\sigma(l|a)+\sigma(o|r)-\sigma(l|r)\bigr],\quad \phi_{R}=\tfrac12\bigl[\sigma(o|a)-\sigma(o|r)+\sigma(l|a)-\sigma(l|r)\bigr],\quad \phi_F+\phi_R=G` (o oracle, l lgbm, a act, r req; checked against the ledger: PM100 `shapley.forecast` + `shapley.runtime` = `shapley.gap`)
- E12 (interaction, H4) `\theta_4=[\sigma(o|a)-\sigma(o|r)]-[\sigma(l|a)-\sigma(l|r)]` (the two terms of E11 each contain half of it)
- E13 (retained share) `\rho=\sigma(\mathrm{lgbm|req})/\sigma(\mathrm{oracle|act})`
Section 4.7
- E14 (percentile interval) `\bigl[\,T^{*}_{(0.025B)},\ T^{*}_{(0.975B)}\bigr]`, B = 2000
- E15 (null-centred one-sided p) `p=\dfrac{1+\#\{b:\ T^{*}_{b}-T\ge T\}}{B+1}` with T the estimate and `T^*_b` the replicate (PR s6; checked in `src/analyze.py` lines 167-169: `dev = bc - est`, `p = (1 + sum(dev >= est)) / (1 + B)`); `1/(B+1)` is the floor, stored value `0.0004997501249375`, written "p < 0.0005" (W23)
- E16 (Holm) `p_{(k)}^{\mathrm{Holm}}=\min\bigl\{1,\ \max_{i\le k}(m-i+1)\,p_{(i)}\bigr\}`, m = 3 (check: PM100 `p_holm` = 3 x `p` at the floor)
Section 5
- E17 (mean start delay) `\bar d=\frac1n\sum_j (s^{\star}_j-a_j)`
- E18 (Spearman rank correlation across the 42 BAs) `r_s=1-\dfrac{6\sum_i d_i^{2}}{n(n^{2}-1)}` (with ties handled by average ranks; say so)
- E19 (absolute effect; PENDING the key of open item 13) `\Delta_{\mathrm{abs}}=\sigma\cdot\bar c_{\mathrm{run\text{-}now}}`, with `\bar c_{\mathrm{run\text{-}now}}=\sum_j E_j^{\mathrm{run\text{-}now}}\big/\bigl(\sum_j P_jR_{\mathrm{act},j}\bigr)` in g CO2 per kWh of job energy
Exploratory (only if the items run): X1 `\hat R=mR_{\mathrm{act}}` for m in {0.25, 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 8}; S2 `\hat c_a` built from data up to `\lfloor a\rfloor-k`.

## 5c. Algorithms (algorithm2e style, numbered lines, vertical scope bars; one small algorithm per component, building to one final algorithm)

- Algorithm 1, Plan (4.1): Input a, S, \hat R, signal \hat c_a (98 intervals), Delta. Output s*. Steps: for each s in S(a,S) compute the integral over [s, s+\hat R] from the interval array; keep the minimum, earliest on ties; return s*. Function header `Plan`.
- Algorithm 2, RuntimeEstimate (4.4): Input job j, mode in {act, req, pred, pred2}, user history. Output \hat R. Steps: act returns R_act; req returns R_req; pred takes the user's last two jobs ended before the submission with state COMPLETED or TIMEOUT, averages, caps at R_req, falls back to R_req; pred2 applies min(R_req, 2 pred). Assertion on end < submit.
- Algorithm 3, AlignAndSample (4.5): Input job pool, BA, period, replicate index, seed. Output placed jobs with arrival a on the BA clock. Steps: draw min(20,000, pool size) jobs; draw a random week of the period; keep the job's local weekday and clock time in the BA's standard local time; drop jobs whose 98 h window touches an unusable hour.
- Algorithm 4, EvaluateAndBootstrap (4.7): Input per-BA daily sums of arm emissions, B, block length 7, seed 7 + S. Output estimates, 95 % intervals, null-centred p, Holm p. Steps: compute T on the data; for b = 1..B resample 7-day blocks within each BA (all 42 BAs kept), recompute T*_b; compute interval and p; apply Holm over H1 to H3.
- Algorithm 5, Study (4.8; final): Input traces, EIA-930 intensity, frozen PR. Output ledger. Steps: build and clean intensity; fit forecasters on TRAIN; select D on DEV; freeze (md5); for each trace, BA, slack, replicate call AlignAndSample; for each arm call Plan with RuntimeEstimate; score emissions; call EvaluateAndBootstrap; write ledger; run secondary analyses and label post-freeze exploratory items separately.
Count: 5 boxes; brief F11 says algorithm boxes occur in 8 of 15 typeset papers (topic-close range 0 to 3) and "more only where a component is introduced"; the owner rule (CLAUDE.md s9) asks for several; panel decides whether Algorithms 1 and 2 merge (open item 27).

