research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:34:W1. Title: owner wants a finding-led title with no negatives and no trailing region or dataset phrase (MP s8). SUSCOM evidence: 14 of 16 titles have at most 16 words; 0 of 16 are finding-led; 9 of 16 use a colon (5 with a method name); descriptive, verb-led and colon-subtitle forms all occur. Rule: at most 16 words, descriptive object or finding clause, no question mark, no acronym beyond HPC (define in the abstract), no method-name colon. A finding clause is allowed but untested in this journal (editor reaction not established, G4); the fallback is a verb-less descriptive title of the 101498 type. Candidates with rationale: LS L8 (six options). Titles must not say that the runtime term exceeds the forecast term in general: it does on PM100 and KIT FH2 and not on F-DATA (figures/FIGURE_FIXES item 2; ledger shapley keys). The owner approves the final title; it stays provisional until all analyses are done (MP s8). [A14 change 5; LS L8]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:64:W25. Per-trace sentences must follow the ledger: the runtime term exceeds the forecast term on PM100 and KIT FH2 and not on F-DATA (keys `TEST.<trace>.w.S12.shapley.runtime` and `.forecast`); H1 to H3 are confirmatory on PM100 only (PREREG s6) and supported there (Holm p keys); on F-DATA and KIT FH2 the same contrasts are secondary replications and are described as such, never as confirmed (controller fix 04:40, flagged by A18); write "on two of three traces", never "runtime matters more than the forecast" without the trace. [LS L3.6; figures/FIGURE_FIXES item 2]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:65:W26. Discussion structure (LS L4.1-L4.2): 6.1 what the 2 x 2 shows; 6.2 implications for operators (log requested and actual runtime, add a history-based predictor), users (walltime accuracy has a carbon value; a limit set too low kills the job), scheduler designers (forecast and runtime are separate levers; the planner is the delay-to-lowest-integral policy, no scheduler is ranked) and signal providers (data latency); 6.3 comparison with earlier work (why 12 h slack on a fixed grid gives smaller savings than the spatial or wind-driven studies; the single-case 101407 headline versus pooled 42 BAs); 6.4 limitations and threats (W9); 6.5 reproducibility (W10). Implications are counterfactual: "would" and "could", never "will be realised". [LS L4; A13 s6; EDITOR R05 q4]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:66:W27. Adopt from earlier work (A13 s6) and say so in text: (1) report the gap and retained share by slack, parallel to 101470's gap by horizon; (2) name oracle|req as the walltime-only cost, parallel to the forecast-only gap of 101470; (3) an injected-error sweep (101216 Fig. 11) only as exploratory X2 (LS L7), never confirmatory; (4) keep run-now and night rule in T4 beside the deployable arm; (5) cite 101498's "runtimes could come from previous runs" as the position the pred arm tests. Answer in Discussion: 101216's zero-error effect has no analogue because the planner is one-stage; code and data release (101470, 101216, 101407, 100950 do not release) mentioned once. [A13 s6]
research/JOURNAL_EXPERT_BRIEF.md:79:F11. Algorithm boxes appear in 8 of 15 typeset papers (topic-close 5 of 10, range 0 to 3): include one (the pre-registered simulation and bootstrap procedure) and more only where a component is introduced. Numbered equations: topic-close median 12, range 0 to 51; plan 12 to 25, numbered and referenced. [A14 change 12]
